Practice makes you perfect.

5
00:00:14,050 --> 00:00:22,240
Have you ever been, you know, maybe in a business meeting or just reading an email from work and you see a phrase like, I'd love to pick your brains.

6
00:00:23,540 --> 00:00:25,240
And for a second you might think, wait, what?

7
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:27,930
Am I some kind of Frankenstein monster?

8
00:00:28,030 --> 00:00:28,870
What are they asking me?

9
00:00:28,950 --> 00:00:29,350
Right.

10
00:00:29,410 --> 00:00:30,970
It can sound a bit odd.

11
00:00:31,050 --> 00:00:33,520
Or someone asks, do you have a minute to hop on a call?

12
00:00:34,540 --> 00:00:37,650
And if you're not used to these, well, these expressions can feel confusing.

13
00:00:38,370 --> 00:00:39,550
Maybe even a bit intimidating.

14
00:00:39,650 --> 00:00:40,350
Definitely.

15
00:00:40,370 --> 00:00:41,610
Okay, so let's unpack this a bit.

16
00:00:42,170 --> 00:00:45,780
The world of business English, it really has its own language, doesn't it?

17
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:46,880
It absolutely does.

18
00:00:46,980 --> 00:00:48,990
It's like a unique dialect.

19
00:00:49,010 --> 00:00:52,340
Yeah, full of these common expressions and acronyms.

20
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:56,760
And they often just don't translate literally at all.

21
00:00:57,590 --> 00:01:02,620
And when you hear one you don't know, it's totally normal to feel, well, maybe less confident.

22
00:01:03,520 --> 00:01:04,520
A little embarrassed even.

23
00:01:04,560 --> 00:01:06,400
Or just confused about what's expected.

24
00:01:06,380 --> 00:01:06,960
Exactly.

25
00:01:07,730 --> 00:01:11,030
So our goal today in this deep dive is to kind of clear all that up.

26
00:01:11,390 --> 00:01:17,970
We're taking a really deep dive into essential business English phrases and acronyms.

27
00:01:18,690 --> 00:01:25,840
And our mission really for you is to give you the tools, you know, the knowledge you need so you can say what you mean accurately.

28
00:01:26,010 --> 00:01:28,110
Understand what people are saying back to you.

29
00:01:28,430 --> 00:01:28,610
Right.

30
00:01:28,670 --> 00:01:34,740
And just generally feel more confident, less awkward in any kind of English speaking professional setting.

31
00:01:34,860 --> 00:01:35,120
Yeah.

32
00:01:35,140 --> 00:01:41,710
Think of it like a shortcut, a way to sound really informed, integrated, like you belong.

33
00:01:41,950 --> 00:01:42,390
Totally.

34
00:01:42,870 --> 00:01:43,990
Getting you on top of your game.

35
00:01:44,330 --> 00:01:45,250
And it's interesting, isn't it?

36
00:01:45,270 --> 00:01:52,480
Because these things that seem quite small linguistically, just a phrase here, an acronym there, they're actually really powerful tools.

37
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:53,060
I thought so.

38
00:01:53,130 --> 00:01:54,810
Well, it's not just knowing the words.

39
00:01:55,050 --> 00:01:57,950
It's understanding the nuances behind them.

40
00:01:58,110 --> 00:02:00,880
You know, the level of politeness or maybe the urgency.

41
00:02:00,900 --> 00:02:02,480
Or an elaborative feel they might be going for.

42
00:02:02,560 --> 00:02:02,960
Exactly.

43
00:02:02,980 --> 00:02:03,940
All those subtleties.

44
00:02:04,310 --> 00:02:09,050
And getting that right, it can seriously impact your interactions, how people see you professionally.

45
00:02:09,070 --> 00:02:10,500
It's pretty fundamental stuff.

46
00:02:10,530 --> 00:02:18,400
Okay, so to make this super practical, really useful right away, we've gone through and picked out, what is it, 20 essential phrases.

47
00:02:18,420 --> 00:02:24,690
20 core phrases, yeah, plus five really crucial acronyms, the ones you're almost certain to bump into at work.

48
00:02:24,830 --> 00:02:27,220
And emails, meetings, just office chat.

49
00:02:27,460 --> 00:02:27,880
Everywhere.

50
00:02:28,180 --> 00:02:31,590
Get ready for some of those aha moments, hopefully.

51
00:02:31,570 --> 00:02:35,380
Because by the end of this, you should feel way more comfortable navigating these conversations.

52
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:37,260
Let's jump in.

53
00:02:37,380 --> 00:02:37,720
Yeah.

54
00:02:38,340 --> 00:02:38,540
All right.

55
00:02:38,560 --> 00:02:41,030
Let's start with how we actually talk to each other at work.

56
00:02:41,090 --> 00:02:44,780
You know, how we collaborate, ask for things, get input.

57
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:48,550
And the first one is just a classic, a master class in being polite.

58
00:02:49,630 --> 00:02:51,010
When you have a minute.

59
00:02:51,180 --> 00:02:53,620
But the slightly more direct, do you have a minute?

60
00:02:54,460 --> 00:02:55,280
You hear these all the time.

61
00:02:55,520 --> 00:02:55,980
All the time.

62
00:02:56,060 --> 00:02:58,090
And it's not just asking, are you free right now?

63
00:02:58,310 --> 00:02:59,010
There's more to it.

64
00:02:59,190 --> 00:02:59,890
Oh, absolutely.

65
00:02:59,990 --> 00:03:08,800
It's a beautiful example of indirectness, which in professional English often signals politeness, respect for the other person's time.

66
00:03:08,860 --> 00:03:11,130
How does it shift the power dynamic, as you mentioned earlier?

67
00:03:11,240 --> 00:03:12,000
Well, think about it.

68
00:03:12,040 --> 00:03:16,470
When you say, when you have a minute, you're acknowledging they're probably busy.

69
00:03:16,730 --> 00:03:18,150
You're respecting their workflow.

70
00:03:18,190 --> 00:03:20,370
You're giving them control over when they respond.

71
00:03:20,390 --> 00:03:20,810
Exactly.

72
00:03:20,830 --> 00:03:22,390
You're not demanding their attention now.

73
00:03:22,910 --> 00:03:26,960
Compare that to just, you know, barging in and saying, look at this.

74
00:03:27,240 --> 00:03:27,400
Right.

75
00:03:27,420 --> 00:03:29,260
It's much less disruptive.

76
00:03:29,340 --> 00:03:29,820
Way less.

77
00:03:30,300 --> 00:03:33,710
An email saying, when you have a minute, could you look at this report?

78
00:03:34,810 --> 00:03:35,810
It builds rapport.

79
00:03:36,770 --> 00:03:38,530
It avoids interrupting them.

80
00:03:38,680 --> 00:03:42,310
And even the do you have a minute version, though more direct.

81
00:03:42,850 --> 00:03:46,730
It's still framed as a question about their availability, not a command.

82
00:03:47,430 --> 00:03:50,060
It still gives them that control, that out if they're swamped.

83
00:03:50,420 --> 00:03:51,040
It's considerate.

84
00:03:51,280 --> 00:03:53,680
That idea of giving control is really key, I think.

85
00:03:54,210 --> 00:03:57,430
Especially when everyone's juggling so much, it sets a really positive tone.

86
00:03:58,090 --> 00:04:02,580
Okay, building on that collaborative spirit, the next one really captures that teamwork feel.

87
00:04:03,840 --> 00:04:05,140
Bounce ideas off of you.

88
00:04:05,280 --> 00:04:06,600
Oh, yeah, I like this one.

89
00:04:06,760 --> 00:04:07,920
The image is great, right?

90
00:04:07,940 --> 00:04:10,960
Like a bouncy ball, you throw an idea out, it comes back, maybe changed a bit.

91
00:04:11,020 --> 00:04:11,700
Yeah, refined.

92
00:04:11,760 --> 00:04:13,200
Or maybe it sparks something totally new.

93
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:16,020
So what's the power in framing a request like that?

94
00:04:16,230 --> 00:04:17,870
Why not just say, give me feedback?

95
00:04:18,570 --> 00:04:22,230
Because bounce ideas off signals something very specific.

96
00:04:23,090 --> 00:04:25,950
It's collaborative, yes, but also low pressure.

97
00:04:26,250 --> 00:04:30,280
You're asking for informal feedback, not some final judgment.

98
00:04:30,880 --> 00:04:33,660
Ah, so it implies the ideas might not be fully formed yet.

99
00:04:33,810 --> 00:04:34,530
Precisely.

100
00:04:34,570 --> 00:04:39,180
You're inviting more of a creative exchange, like using someone as a sounding board.

101
00:04:39,460 --> 00:04:40,540
It suggests you're open.

102
00:04:40,720 --> 00:04:42,520
You don't expect perfect answers right away.

103
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:45,000
That makes sense, especially early on in a project.

104
00:04:45,310 --> 00:04:45,820
Definitely.

105
00:04:45,990 --> 00:04:52,730
Like in the initial stages or when you're brainstorming or just trying to figure out a problem, it creates a safer space psychologically.

106
00:04:53,010 --> 00:04:54,560
So people might share bolder ideas.

107
00:04:54,860 --> 00:04:55,440
Exactly.

108
00:04:55,480 --> 00:04:58,160
Ideas that might not come out in a more formal review.

109
00:04:58,200 --> 00:05:00,580
Like, ah, I'm kind of stuck on this new marketing angle.

110
00:05:00,660 --> 00:05:02,260
Mind if I bounce some ideas off you.

111
00:05:02,330 --> 00:05:03,650
And you mentioned you can drop the of?

112
00:05:04,010 --> 00:05:06,100
Yeah, sometimes you'll hear a bounce of ideas off you.

113
00:05:06,120 --> 00:05:07,940
A little more informal, but same meaning.

114
00:05:08,310 --> 00:05:10,610
It's really an invitation to just explore together.

115
00:05:10,830 --> 00:05:11,370
No pressure.

116
00:05:11,860 --> 00:05:12,160
Love that.

117
00:05:12,240 --> 00:05:13,600
Okay, next phrase.

118
00:05:14,940 --> 00:05:17,570
This one always makes me pause for a second because it sounds...

119
00:05:18,120 --> 00:05:19,810
Well, a big graphic if you take it literally.

120
00:05:20,570 --> 00:05:21,190
Pick your brains.

121
00:05:21,490 --> 00:05:22,570
Huh.

122
00:05:22,590 --> 00:05:24,850
Yeah, it does sound a little invasive.

123
00:05:24,930 --> 00:05:25,150
Right.

124
00:05:25,470 --> 00:05:27,030
Are you planning some kind of operation?

125
00:05:27,940 --> 00:05:31,340
But in business, it actually means something very respectful, doesn't it?

126
00:05:31,580 --> 00:05:32,340
It really does.

127
00:05:32,460 --> 00:05:35,800
It's a curious idiom for sure, but it's brilliant.

128
00:05:36,860 --> 00:05:43,430
Picking someone's brains is a direct but very respectful way to ask for expert input.

129
00:05:43,450 --> 00:05:45,210
So you're acknowledging they know more than you.

130
00:05:45,230 --> 00:05:46,030
Exactly.

131
00:05:46,050 --> 00:05:55,140
You're recognizing their experience, their specific knowledge, their unique viewpoint, and you're respectfully asking for their advice or insights based on that expertise.

132
00:05:55,160 --> 00:05:56,740
So it's kind of flattering in a way.

133
00:05:57,110 --> 00:05:58,030
It absolutely can be.

134
00:05:58,250 --> 00:06:01,330
You're essentially saying your knowledge is so valuable, I want to learn from you.

135
00:06:01,350 --> 00:06:05,780
It's great for mentorship situations or tapping into that institutional knowledge.

136
00:06:05,800 --> 00:06:07,840
Like asking someone who's been at the company a long time.

137
00:06:08,060 --> 00:06:08,840
Perfect example.

138
00:06:09,060 --> 00:06:10,960
Instead of just how does this place work?

139
00:06:11,380 --> 00:06:13,390
You could say, I know you've been here 10 years.

140
00:06:13,430 --> 00:06:15,690
I'd love to pick your brains about the company culture.

141
00:06:16,150 --> 00:06:18,480
It shows deference, appreciation.

142
00:06:18,500 --> 00:06:19,580
Makes the person feel valued.

143
00:06:19,760 --> 00:06:20,240
Totally.

144
00:06:20,780 --> 00:06:25,510
Or, you know, could we maybe hop on a call so I can pick your brains about the Q3 strategy?

145
00:06:25,950 --> 00:06:26,050
Yeah.

146
00:06:26,070 --> 00:06:29,660
It signals you really value their specific thinking on that topic.

147
00:06:30,490 --> 00:06:32,030
And speaking of hopping on calls.

148
00:06:32,090 --> 00:06:32,430
Yeah.

149
00:06:32,770 --> 00:06:33,770
Perfect segue.

150
00:06:34,350 --> 00:06:36,600
Our next phrase, hop on a call.

151
00:06:36,980 --> 00:06:37,620
Ubiquitous.

152
00:06:37,940 --> 00:06:38,960
Totally everywhere now.

153
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:39,280
Yeah.

154
00:06:39,780 --> 00:06:43,250
It's the standard way to suggest a quick, unplanned chat.

155
00:06:43,710 --> 00:06:45,330
Could be phone, could be Zoom, whatever.

156
00:06:46,290 --> 00:06:49,100
What does that phrase imply about the conversation itself?

157
00:06:49,580 --> 00:06:51,260
Is it different from scheduling a meeting?

158
00:06:51,240 --> 00:06:55,170
Yeah, it usually implies something quicker, more agile, more immediate than a formal meeting.

159
00:06:55,790 --> 00:07:00,130
It suggests that typing back and forth in emails is maybe too slow or getting complicated.

160
00:07:00,150 --> 00:07:01,950
Right, when you need that real-time clarification.

161
00:07:02,070 --> 00:07:02,710
Exactly.

162
00:07:02,730 --> 00:07:07,240
Think about urgent issues or explaining something complex where you need immediate back and forth.

163
00:07:07,680 --> 00:07:12,800
It signals you want direct, real-time interaction to cut through delays or misunderstandings.

164
00:07:12,820 --> 00:07:14,960
Like, hey, do you have a minute to hop on a call?

165
00:07:15,430 --> 00:07:16,610
The client has some questions.

166
00:07:16,740 --> 00:07:17,120
Perfect.

167
00:07:17,340 --> 00:07:19,610
Or if an email chain is just going in circles, you know what?

168
00:07:19,900 --> 00:07:21,510
It might be easier to just talk this through.

169
00:07:21,570 --> 00:07:23,070
Can we hop on a call for five minutes?

170
00:07:23,290 --> 00:07:24,950
It's about speed and clarity.

171
00:07:25,130 --> 00:07:25,460
Got it.

172
00:07:26,080 --> 00:07:30,120
Quick, agile, real-time, cuts to the noise.

173
00:07:30,140 --> 00:07:36,960
Okay, final one in this section about collaborating a real, classic, fundamental for generating ideas.

174
00:07:37,040 --> 00:07:39,820
Ah, the brainstorm session.

175
00:07:39,850 --> 00:07:40,570
The classic.

176
00:07:40,650 --> 00:07:43,730
It's universally understood for that group idea generation thing.

177
00:07:44,250 --> 00:07:48,440
When a team says they're going to brainstorm, what's the main principle they should be following?

178
00:07:48,900 --> 00:07:57,480
The absolute core principle, the thing that makes brainstorming work, is getting all the ideas out there, putting everything on the table initially without judgment.

179
00:07:57,590 --> 00:07:59,700
So quantity over quality at first.

180
00:07:59,720 --> 00:08:00,160
Exactly.

181
00:08:00,180 --> 00:08:01,360
You're aiming for volume.

182
00:08:01,660 --> 00:08:09,530
It's about creating a safe, judgment-free zone where people feel comfortable throwing out initial thoughts, no matter how wild or simple they might seem.

183
00:08:09,630 --> 00:08:10,550
Why is that important?

184
00:08:10,570 --> 00:08:12,960
Because it encourages participation from everyone.

185
00:08:13,520 --> 00:08:20,110
It helps generate a really wide, diverse pool of options before you start narrowing things down or criticizing.

186
00:08:20,170 --> 00:08:20,970
It's the launch pad.

187
00:08:21,070 --> 00:08:23,420
Prevents good ideas from getting shot down too early.

188
00:08:23,390 --> 00:08:24,000
Precisely.

189
00:08:24,180 --> 00:08:26,860
You'll often hear it used like in the source example.

190
00:08:27,320 --> 00:08:31,160
Okay team, tomorrow morning we're going to brainstorm ideas for the new campaign.

191
00:08:31,220 --> 00:08:35,770
It's about opening the floodgates first and saving the evaluation for later.

192
00:08:36,170 --> 00:08:46,100
OK, moving on from collaboration, let's talk about managing our work, tasks, deadlines, making sure information gets to the right people efficiently.

193
00:08:46,140 --> 00:08:48,100
Yeah, the nuts and bolts of getting things done.

194
00:08:48,320 --> 00:08:48,800
Exactly.

195
00:08:49,250 --> 00:08:54,230
So first phrase here is simple, but really useful for setting expectations about timing.

196
00:08:54,970 --> 00:08:55,670
First thing in the morning.

197
00:08:56,890 --> 00:09:01,860
And a little language note, I've noticed sometimes people say first thing in the morning, but usually it's just first thing, right?

198
00:09:01,880 --> 00:09:03,120
Like I'll do it first thing.

199
00:09:03,100 --> 00:09:05,630
Yeah, often just first thing or first thing tomorrow morning.

200
00:09:05,650 --> 00:09:08,570
The core idea is the professional impact it has.

201
00:09:08,830 --> 00:09:10,170
It's a strong commitment.

202
00:09:10,610 --> 00:09:16,240
When you say you'll do something first thing in the morning, you're giving a very clear, pretty precise time frame.

203
00:09:16,260 --> 00:09:17,470
You're saying it's a high priority.

204
00:09:17,830 --> 00:09:21,430
So it implies it'll be one of the very first tasks you tackle.

205
00:09:21,660 --> 00:09:22,890
That's the implication, yes.

206
00:09:23,370 --> 00:09:25,290
It builds trust, shows reliability.

207
00:09:25,350 --> 00:09:33,580
If your manager asks for something and you say, can't do it now, but I'll get it to you first thing in the morning, they know it's on your immediate radar for the next day.

208
00:09:33,800 --> 00:09:34,570
Like the example.

209
00:09:35,210 --> 00:09:37,970
Sorry, can't finish the report today, but I'll do it first thing.

210
00:09:38,150 --> 00:09:38,550
Perfect.

211
00:09:38,810 --> 00:09:39,690
It's a clear promise.

212
00:09:39,910 --> 00:09:48,990
It means at the very start of the next business day, without needing to commit to like 8.01 a.m. exactly, it manages expectations well.

213
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,610
Yeah, that clarity is great when you can't deliver instantly.

214
00:09:52,450 --> 00:09:54,570
Okay, next, for quick messages, we've got this pair.

215
00:09:55,030 --> 00:09:57,420
Shoot off an email or fire off an email.

216
00:09:57,720 --> 00:09:58,760
Hmm, the speedy ones.

217
00:09:59,080 --> 00:10:00,720
They really sound fast, don't they?

218
00:10:00,780 --> 00:10:01,660
Like a quick action.

219
00:10:02,110 --> 00:10:03,950
What kind of message is using these send?

220
00:10:04,250 --> 00:10:06,740
They absolutely signal speed and brevity.

221
00:10:06,770 --> 00:10:12,450
It's interesting how those verbs, shoot and fire, convey that sense of urgency, that efficiency.

222
00:10:12,760 --> 00:10:15,260
So it's not for a long, carefully written email.

223
00:10:15,450 --> 00:10:16,270
Generally not.

224
00:10:16,370 --> 00:10:20,530
It's more about getting essential info out quickly, maybe something time sensitive.

225
00:10:21,390 --> 00:10:27,200
In today's world with email overload, it signals you're taking quick action, focusing on the key message.

226
00:10:27,720 --> 00:10:31,620
Like I'm just going to shoot off an email with those questions before the client call.

227
00:10:31,800 --> 00:10:32,300
Exactly.

228
00:10:32,580 --> 00:10:33,490
Or you could combine it.

229
00:10:33,750 --> 00:10:36,830
Just shoot me an email with the details and I'll look at it first thing.

230
00:10:37,250 --> 00:10:41,820
It suggests a proactive, no-nonsense approach, a quick, necessary message.

231
00:10:42,060 --> 00:10:42,640
Got it.

232
00:10:42,660 --> 00:10:43,620
Efficient and direct.

233
00:10:45,320 --> 00:10:47,010
Okay, now, keeping people informed.

234
00:10:47,270 --> 00:10:49,650
This phrase is absolutely vital for transparency.

235
00:10:50,170 --> 00:10:51,520
Keep someone in the loop.

236
00:10:51,540 --> 00:10:52,940
Essential for teamwork.

237
00:10:52,960 --> 00:10:55,380
I always picture that continuous circle of information.

238
00:10:55,950 --> 00:10:56,930
Nobody's left outside.

239
00:10:56,990 --> 00:10:57,890
Why is this so important?

240
00:10:57,920 --> 00:10:58,720
Oh, it's fundamental.

241
00:10:59,120 --> 00:11:04,550
Keeping people in the loop means making sure all the relevant people know what's going on, updates, progress, issues.

242
00:11:04,620 --> 00:11:05,680
Prevents confusion.

243
00:11:05,860 --> 00:11:06,240
Totally.

244
00:11:06,540 --> 00:11:11,410
It stops miscommunication, stops people duplicating work, lets everyone make informed decisions.

245
00:11:11,670 --> 00:11:14,580
It builds that sense of inclusion, shared ownership.

246
00:11:14,590 --> 00:11:19,380
Without it, people feel isolated, maybe make bad calls because they didn't have the info.

247
00:11:19,500 --> 00:11:19,940
Exactly.

248
00:11:19,980 --> 00:11:21,200
Like, hey, I missed the meeting.

249
00:11:21,220 --> 00:11:22,200
Could you keep me in the loop?

250
00:11:22,240 --> 00:11:23,220
Just forward the notes.

251
00:11:23,340 --> 00:11:27,450
Or from the top down, the boss wants you to keep her in the loop on this project.

252
00:11:27,570 --> 00:11:27,770
Right.

253
00:11:28,230 --> 00:11:32,400
It's about creating that shared understanding, making sure everyone has the context they need.

254
00:11:32,560 --> 00:11:33,020
Makes sense.

255
00:11:34,560 --> 00:11:34,860
Okay.

256
00:11:35,560 --> 00:11:41,010
After a big project or event, there's this crucial step that's more than just a summary.

257
00:11:41,690 --> 00:11:42,590
Debrief.

258
00:11:43,110 --> 00:11:47,640
And this could be a noun, let's have a debrief, or a verb, we need a debrief.

259
00:11:47,660 --> 00:11:49,700
Why is it more than just a recap?

260
00:11:49,720 --> 00:11:51,080
Because it's really about learning.

261
00:11:51,120 --> 00:11:54,040
A debrief is a structured process for reflection.

262
00:11:54,180 --> 00:11:57,470
It's how organizations learn from experience and actually improve.

263
00:11:58,110 --> 00:11:59,770
So analyzing what happened.

264
00:11:59,790 --> 00:12:00,890
Yes, systematically.

265
00:12:01,050 --> 00:12:01,770
What went well?

266
00:12:02,070 --> 00:12:02,390
Why?

267
00:12:02,570 --> 00:12:03,410
What didn't go well?

268
00:12:03,570 --> 00:12:05,250
What did we learn from the challenges?

269
00:12:05,590 --> 00:12:09,880
And crucially, what specific things can we do differently next time?

270
00:12:10,090 --> 00:12:12,420
Turning experience into actionable insights.

271
00:12:12,500 --> 00:12:12,900
Exactly.

272
00:12:13,100 --> 00:12:16,480
That's why it's a cornerstone of good project management of continuous improvement.

273
00:12:16,800 --> 00:12:18,540
It stops teams from repeating mistakes.

274
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:19,730
Like the examples.

275
00:12:20,230 --> 00:12:22,270
After the project, let's have a meeting to debrief.

276
00:12:23,070 --> 00:12:26,130
Or we had a debrief and decided to change our process.

277
00:12:26,430 --> 00:12:26,650
Right.

278
00:12:26,790 --> 00:12:29,280
It links the learning directly to future action.

279
00:12:29,620 --> 00:12:30,380
Very powerful.

280
00:12:30,700 --> 00:12:30,960
Okay.

281
00:12:31,140 --> 00:12:32,280
Final phrase in this section.

282
00:12:33,120 --> 00:12:35,450
Focusing on making things better, more efficient.

283
00:12:35,470 --> 00:12:35,970
Streamline.

284
00:12:36,070 --> 00:12:38,130
Ah, the quest for efficiency.

285
00:12:38,350 --> 00:12:38,570
Right.

286
00:12:38,710 --> 00:12:45,280
It's about moving away from complicated or messy processes towards something smooth, organized, effective.

287
00:12:46,480 --> 00:12:47,120
Optimization.

288
00:12:47,680 --> 00:12:48,160
Exactly.

289
00:12:48,210 --> 00:12:53,250
And what's key here is that streamlining isn't just about saving time, although it usually does that.

290
00:12:53,910 --> 00:12:57,380
It's fundamentally about optimizing how you use resources.

291
00:12:57,550 --> 00:12:58,490
Reducing costs.

292
00:12:59,270 --> 00:13:00,190
Improving productivity.

293
00:13:00,450 --> 00:13:01,110
All of the above.

294
00:13:01,430 --> 00:13:02,930
Think about a chaotic workflow.

295
00:13:02,950 --> 00:13:03,870
Emails everywhere.

296
00:13:03,910 --> 00:13:04,790
Multiple sign-offs.

297
00:13:04,850 --> 00:13:05,860
Manual steps.

298
00:13:05,880 --> 00:13:07,900
Streamlining gets rid of the unnecessary stuff.

299
00:13:07,980 --> 00:13:10,280
Maybe automates parts, clarifies the steps.

300
00:13:10,300 --> 00:13:11,480
Makes it easier for everyone.

301
00:13:11,500 --> 00:13:14,420
And often leads to real savings and happier employees too.

302
00:13:14,680 --> 00:13:19,910
Like, if we streamline this onboarding process, we'll cut costs and get new hires up to speed faster.

303
00:13:20,070 --> 00:13:23,990
Or the other example, the company streamlined by reducing management layers.

304
00:13:24,090 --> 00:13:26,060
Yeah, making things leaner, more direct.

305
00:13:26,400 --> 00:13:31,600
It's about purposeful, strategic optimization for better results with less friction.

306
00:13:32,240 --> 00:13:32,440
All right.

307
00:13:32,460 --> 00:13:33,300
Let's shift gears a bit.

308
00:13:33,540 --> 00:13:40,100
Now we're looking at phrases tied more to strategic thinking and managing big projects.

309
00:13:40,170 --> 00:13:40,420
Yeah.

310
00:13:40,720 --> 00:13:42,680
Especially when you need everyone pulling together.

311
00:13:42,800 --> 00:13:43,620
High level stuff.

312
00:13:43,640 --> 00:13:44,360
Yeah.

313
00:13:44,380 --> 00:13:44,960
Big picture.

314
00:13:45,160 --> 00:13:47,410
And kicking us off is one with a great backstory.

315
00:13:47,870 --> 00:13:48,770
All hands on deck.

316
00:13:49,170 --> 00:13:50,390
It comes from sailing ships, right?

317
00:13:50,410 --> 00:13:50,810
It does.

318
00:13:50,930 --> 00:13:56,140
Literally meant every sailor needed to be on the deck, usually for an emergency or a critical maneuver.

319
00:13:56,380 --> 00:13:57,950
So how does that translate to the office?

320
00:13:58,060 --> 00:13:59,240
Pretty directly, actually.

321
00:13:59,280 --> 00:14:06,050
In business, all hands on deck signals a critical situation or a high priority task where everyone's contribution is needed.

322
00:14:06,550 --> 00:14:07,010
Urgently.

323
00:14:07,070 --> 00:14:08,200
Like, drop what you're doing.

324
00:14:08,220 --> 00:14:09,320
This is the focus now.

325
00:14:09,400 --> 00:14:10,160
Often, yeah.

326
00:14:10,460 --> 00:14:13,140
It implies urgency, high stakes.

327
00:14:13,160 --> 00:14:15,090
Normal work might need to pause or shift.

328
00:14:15,250 --> 00:14:20,150
So examples like, we need all hands on deck to hit this client deadline.

329
00:14:20,170 --> 00:14:20,580
Perfect.

330
00:14:20,890 --> 00:14:24,020
Or, the CEO's called an all-hands meeting for Thursday.

331
00:14:24,660 --> 00:14:27,360
It means unity, maximum effort, focus.

332
00:14:27,560 --> 00:14:27,900
Got it.

333
00:14:28,470 --> 00:14:29,710
A powerful call to action.

334
00:14:30,030 --> 00:14:34,290
Okay, next, a word that's absolutely crucial for making sure everyone's on the same page.

335
00:14:34,870 --> 00:14:35,700
Align.

336
00:14:36,110 --> 00:14:40,160
And just a quick pronunciation reminder that G is silent.

337
00:14:40,880 --> 00:14:41,900
A-L-I-G-N.

338
00:14:42,380 --> 00:14:45,190
It means being in agreement, supporting the same goal.

339
00:14:45,630 --> 00:14:46,890
Why is this so fundamental?

340
00:14:46,910 --> 00:14:48,310
Oh, alignment is everything, really.

341
00:14:48,610 --> 00:14:49,950
Connecting it to the bigger picture.

342
00:14:50,030 --> 00:14:52,940
It's foundational for strategy, for teamwork, for partnerships.

343
00:14:52,960 --> 00:14:53,780
If things aren't aligned.

344
00:14:53,900 --> 00:14:54,580
You get chaos.

345
00:14:54,940 --> 00:14:55,680
Wasted effort.

346
00:14:56,120 --> 00:14:59,050
Imagine a project that doesn't align with the company's main goals.

347
00:14:59,290 --> 00:15:00,590
It's probably going to get canceled, right?

348
00:15:00,950 --> 00:15:02,390
Because it's pulling in the wrong direction.

349
00:15:02,370 --> 00:15:02,910
Makes sense.

350
00:15:03,170 --> 00:15:08,020
And companies actively try to align with others, partners, allies who share similar goals.

351
00:15:08,140 --> 00:15:09,100
It creates synergy.

352
00:15:09,480 --> 00:15:12,620
It's about making sure all the pieces are moving together purposefully.

353
00:15:13,040 --> 00:15:16,630
Like the example, that project doesn't align with our goals, so we're not doing it.

354
00:15:17,110 --> 00:15:17,510
Exactly.

355
00:15:17,730 --> 00:15:21,850
Or our company aligns itself with organizations focused on sustainability.

356
00:15:22,460 --> 00:15:25,140
It provides direction, ensures consistency.

357
00:15:25,340 --> 00:15:26,140
Very important.

358
00:15:26,720 --> 00:15:31,450
Okay, now for businesses that want to grow, maybe grow fast, this next word is key.

359
00:15:32,070 --> 00:15:32,810
Scalable.

360
00:15:33,210 --> 00:15:34,990
Hugely important, especially today.

361
00:15:35,250 --> 00:15:43,970
It describes something, an idea, a business that can grow bigger, handle more volume, increase profits, without the costs or effort increasing at the same rate.

362
00:15:44,210 --> 00:15:44,450
Right.

363
00:15:44,690 --> 00:15:45,550
Leveraging growth.

364
00:15:45,790 --> 00:15:47,110
That's the core idea.

365
00:15:47,170 --> 00:15:52,100
And the crucial question it raises is, how does sagalability affect long-term potential?

366
00:15:52,560 --> 00:15:54,280
Why are investors obsessed with it?

367
00:15:54,510 --> 00:15:56,200
Because it means potentially huge returns.

368
00:15:56,440 --> 00:15:56,940
Exactly.

369
00:15:56,960 --> 00:16:02,100
A scalable business can reach a much bigger market, maybe globally, without its costs exploding.

370
00:16:02,360 --> 00:16:04,950
The underlying system can handle way more demand.

371
00:16:05,030 --> 00:16:06,570
The source had a great example with teaching.

372
00:16:06,690 --> 00:16:08,290
Yeah, the classroom versus YouTube.

373
00:16:08,470 --> 00:16:12,220
Teaching 30 people in a room, not very scalable, but an online course.

374
00:16:12,620 --> 00:16:14,900
You can reach millions with roughly the same initial effort.

375
00:16:15,040 --> 00:16:16,100
That really clarifies it.

376
00:16:16,400 --> 00:16:18,460
Exponential growth potential versus linear.

377
00:16:18,540 --> 00:16:19,130
Precisely.

378
00:16:19,150 --> 00:16:22,970
This startup is small now, but the model is highly scalable.

379
00:16:23,790 --> 00:16:25,690
That's music to an investor's ears.

380
00:16:25,880 --> 00:16:26,410
I bet.

381
00:16:26,450 --> 00:16:30,710
OK, now something we can probably all relate to in meetings or complex projects.

382
00:16:30,970 --> 00:16:31,210
Yeah.

383
00:16:31,530 --> 00:16:34,540
Getting in the weeds or being lost in the weeds.

384
00:16:34,660 --> 00:16:35,060
Oh, yes.

385
00:16:35,460 --> 00:16:36,420
The danger zone.

386
00:16:36,680 --> 00:16:41,690
It means getting so bogged down in tiny details that you lose track of the main point, the overall goal.

387
00:16:42,110 --> 00:16:45,210
Like you can't see the forest for the trees or rather the weeds.

388
00:16:45,510 --> 00:16:46,770
It's such a perfect metaphor.

389
00:16:46,840 --> 00:16:48,660
And it's a common trap, isn't it?

390
00:16:49,400 --> 00:16:51,840
While detail is good, getting lost in it?

391
00:16:52,480 --> 00:16:55,960
That leads to delays, bad decisions, missing the whole point.

392
00:16:56,180 --> 00:16:58,230
So how do you handle it when a discussion goes there?

393
00:16:58,590 --> 00:17:00,150
You need strategies to refocus.

394
00:17:00,630 --> 00:17:03,730
Gently but firmly, pull things back to the core objectives.

395
00:17:03,990 --> 00:17:05,630
Remind everyone what the main goal is.

396
00:17:05,930 --> 00:17:09,940
Like, OK, team, interesting point, but let's not get lost in the weeds here.

397
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,220
We need to focus on the main client deliverable.

398
00:17:12,560 --> 00:17:13,080
Exactly.

399
00:17:13,120 --> 00:17:20,970
It's a polite way to redirect, keep things on track, and stop time being wasted on things that ultimately might not matter that much for the big picture.

400
00:17:21,100 --> 00:17:22,880
Good skill to have.

401
00:17:22,900 --> 00:17:30,690
And speaking of keeping discussions on track when things do veer off or you need to manage the agenda, this next phrase is super useful.

402
00:17:31,030 --> 00:17:31,690
Circle back.

403
00:17:32,010 --> 00:17:33,760
A meeting facilitator's best friend.

404
00:17:33,920 --> 00:17:34,120
Right.

405
00:17:34,580 --> 00:17:37,180
It means let's return to this topic or question later.

406
00:17:37,880 --> 00:17:41,050
We acknowledge it's important, but we'll deal with it at a better time.

407
00:17:41,990 --> 00:17:43,330
How does it help keep focus?

408
00:17:43,600 --> 00:17:45,000
It's incredibly effective.

409
00:17:45,320 --> 00:17:48,080
It's a polite, professional way to manage the agenda.

410
00:17:48,480 --> 00:17:54,370
You validate the point someone raised showing respect, but you defer the deep dive to keep the current discussion moving.

411
00:17:54,550 --> 00:17:56,650
Prevents tangents from derailing everything.

412
00:17:56,670 --> 00:17:57,430
Exactly.

413
00:17:57,470 --> 00:18:00,150
And it ensures the idea isn't just forgotten.

414
00:18:00,370 --> 00:18:03,630
You can also use it to gently remind someone about something unresolved.

415
00:18:04,290 --> 00:18:06,700
Just wanted to circle back to my question from last week.

416
00:18:06,720 --> 00:18:07,300
We're in a meeting.

417
00:18:07,520 --> 00:18:08,340
Great point, Sarah.

418
00:18:08,780 --> 00:18:11,480
Can we circle back to that after we finish this agenda item?

419
00:18:11,500 --> 00:18:12,200
Perfect.

420
00:18:12,180 --> 00:18:14,280
It maintains control politely.

421
00:18:14,580 --> 00:18:17,130
Okay, very similar, but maybe slightly different.

422
00:18:17,670 --> 00:18:18,290
Put a pin in it?

423
00:18:19,030 --> 00:18:19,630
What does this mean?

424
00:18:20,130 --> 00:18:22,230
I always imagine sticking a pin in a note on a cork board.

425
00:18:22,670 --> 00:18:23,840
That's a great way to think about it.

426
00:18:24,340 --> 00:18:29,260
It's basically a mental placeholder, a reminder to return to an idea or topic later.

427
00:18:29,540 --> 00:18:31,490
So how is it different from Circleback?

428
00:18:31,510 --> 00:18:32,670
Is there a nuance?

429
00:18:33,050 --> 00:18:34,510
There's a subtle one, yeah.

430
00:18:35,540 --> 00:18:42,150
Often, put a pin in it is used more for deferring a specific idea or suggestion that's just popped up.

431
00:18:42,170 --> 00:18:43,300
Like, that's a great idea.

432
00:18:43,320 --> 00:18:44,680
Let's put a pin in it for now.

433
00:18:45,180 --> 00:18:47,190
It acknowledges the value but parks it.

434
00:18:47,470 --> 00:18:51,520
Whereas circle back might be for a broader topic or a discussion that's already started.

435
00:18:51,630 --> 00:18:52,520
Generally, yes.

436
00:18:52,780 --> 00:19:01,370
Circle back often implies returning to an ongoing thread or a planned item, while put a pin in it is more like capturing a new spark to examine later.

437
00:19:01,770 --> 00:19:05,920
Both manage flow, but pin feels more about capturing a single concept.

438
00:19:06,080 --> 00:19:07,620
Like, good thought on the new software.

439
00:19:07,920 --> 00:19:10,640
Let's put a pin in it and discuss it at the strategy meeting next week.

440
00:19:10,780 --> 00:19:11,260
Exactly.

441
00:19:11,400 --> 00:19:14,370
It queues up the idea without disrupting the current flow.

442
00:19:14,550 --> 00:19:15,710
All right, let's switch focus again.

443
00:19:16,500 --> 00:19:22,300
Now to some really common expressions we use to talk about, well, feelings at work or the kinds of people we work with.

444
00:19:22,440 --> 00:19:23,700
The human side of things.

445
00:19:23,750 --> 00:19:24,170
Totally.

446
00:19:24,630 --> 00:19:31,300
And first up, something probably everyone listening has felt, having a case of the Mondays or just sighing Mondays.

447
00:19:31,580 --> 00:19:32,000
Am I right?

448
00:19:32,160 --> 00:19:32,440
Huh.

449
00:19:33,180 --> 00:19:35,120
The universal Monday morning feeling.

450
00:19:35,220 --> 00:19:38,790
It's that lack of motivation, maybe feeling a bit grumpy after the weekend.

451
00:19:38,810 --> 00:19:39,430
We've all been there.

452
00:19:39,450 --> 00:19:40,090
We really have.

453
00:19:40,150 --> 00:19:42,390
And what's interesting is how it functions socially.

454
00:19:42,450 --> 00:19:49,160
It's a safe, lighthearted way to express that minor frustration without actually complaining about your job or colleagues.

455
00:19:49,300 --> 00:19:50,000
It's relatable.

456
00:19:50,180 --> 00:19:50,720
Very.

457
00:19:50,840 --> 00:19:54,500
It taps into that shared cultural thing, the end of weekend side.

458
00:19:54,610 --> 00:19:56,430
It actually builds a little camaraderie.

459
00:19:56,570 --> 00:19:58,230
Like, ugh, I cannot get started today.

460
00:19:58,450 --> 00:19:58,710
Yeah.

461
00:19:58,730 --> 00:20:00,010
Definitely got a case of the Mondays.

462
00:20:00,250 --> 00:20:00,450
Right.

463
00:20:00,630 --> 00:20:01,820
Or if the coffee machine breaks.

464
00:20:02,190 --> 00:20:04,100
Typical Mondays, am I right?

465
00:20:04,700 --> 00:20:06,880
It's a shared groan, often humorous.

466
00:20:06,920 --> 00:20:08,460
Yeah, definitely softens the edges.

467
00:20:09,200 --> 00:20:12,430
Okay, now for the complete opposite end of the work ethic spectrum.

468
00:20:13,410 --> 00:20:15,860
A really negative term you never want to apply to you.

469
00:20:16,640 --> 00:20:17,400
A slacker.

470
00:20:17,730 --> 00:20:19,860
Yeah, that's a harsh one.

471
00:20:19,880 --> 00:20:23,650
It means someone lazy, avoids work, lets others do the heavy lifting.

472
00:20:24,130 --> 00:20:27,170
What's the actual impact when someone gets this reputation?

473
00:20:27,280 --> 00:20:31,250
The impact is huge and overwhelmingly negative for the team.

474
00:20:31,930 --> 00:20:33,170
It's not just mild criticism.

475
00:20:33,250 --> 00:20:37,660
It carries real frustration because a slacker undermines the whole team's effort.

476
00:20:37,760 --> 00:20:39,120
They create more work for others.

477
00:20:39,340 --> 00:20:40,040
Absolutely.

478
00:20:40,200 --> 00:20:46,630
It messes up workload balance, breeds resentment, hurts morale, and can even risk project failure.

479
00:20:46,810 --> 00:20:49,060
It signals a total lack of accountability.

480
00:20:49,170 --> 00:20:50,980
The examples really hit home.

481
00:20:50,960 --> 00:20:53,140
He's such a slacker, always late with his part.

482
00:20:53,200 --> 00:20:55,020
Or the classic group project scenario.

483
00:20:55,220 --> 00:20:58,390
There's always one slacker who expects everyone else to do the work.

484
00:20:59,050 --> 00:21:00,930
It highlights that unfair burden.

485
00:21:01,550 --> 00:21:02,840
Definitely a label to avoid.

486
00:21:03,180 --> 00:21:03,620
Seriously.

487
00:21:04,120 --> 00:21:05,260
Okay, much more positive now.

488
00:21:05,660 --> 00:21:07,020
The polar opposite of a slacker.

489
00:21:08,340 --> 00:21:09,090
A workhorse.

490
00:21:09,200 --> 00:21:11,260
Ah, the heroes of the office.

491
00:21:11,280 --> 00:21:16,790
Yeah, someone super hardworking, diligent, reliable, gets stuff done, often goes the extra mile.

492
00:21:17,230 --> 00:21:19,020
What kind of reputation does this earn you?

493
00:21:19,160 --> 00:21:20,960
It earns you immense respect.

494
00:21:20,980 --> 00:21:23,720
Workhorse has really strong positive connotations.

495
00:21:24,140 --> 00:21:28,390
It highlights dedication, reliability, consistent effort, all highly valued.

496
00:21:28,370 --> 00:21:29,510
He's with people you can count on.

497
00:21:29,910 --> 00:21:30,270
Always.

498
00:21:30,370 --> 00:21:34,680
They often do the essential, maybe less glamorous tasks without fuss.

499
00:21:34,740 --> 00:21:35,520
They just deliver.

500
00:21:36,180 --> 00:21:41,380
And connecting that to careers, being seen as a workhorse often leads to recognition, promotions.

501
00:21:41,940 --> 00:21:42,540
They're valued.

502
00:21:42,560 --> 00:21:44,330
Like he's a real workhorse for this team.

503
00:21:44,590 --> 00:21:45,670
Always gets it done.

504
00:21:45,710 --> 00:21:47,290
Or she totally deserves that promotion.

505
00:21:47,330 --> 00:21:48,670
She's been a workhorse for years.

506
00:21:49,450 --> 00:21:54,280
It shows deep appreciation for sustained quality work.

507
00:21:54,410 --> 00:21:55,710
You definitely want to be the workhorse.

508
00:21:56,170 --> 00:21:56,270
Okay.

509
00:21:56,290 --> 00:21:56,830
Okay.

510
00:21:56,850 --> 00:22:00,340
Last one in this section describes a management style that drives people crazy.

511
00:22:01,160 --> 00:22:02,240
Micromanage.

512
00:22:02,260 --> 00:22:04,300
The dreaded micromanagement.

513
00:22:04,320 --> 00:22:04,420
Yeah.

514
00:22:04,440 --> 00:22:10,970
When a boss controls every tiny little detail of what you do, how you do it, often kills autonomy, right?

515
00:22:11,110 --> 00:22:11,590
Absolutely.

516
00:22:11,670 --> 00:22:13,260
And the consequences are pretty severe.

517
00:22:13,320 --> 00:22:19,600
It really raises the question, what does that constant oversight do to trust, creativity, independence?

518
00:22:19,620 --> 00:22:20,680
It stifles them.

519
00:22:20,660 --> 00:22:21,350
Completely.

520
00:22:21,370 --> 00:22:23,670
Employees feel distrusted, undervalued.

521
00:22:23,990 --> 00:22:27,470
Their ability to take initiative or solve problems gets crushed.

522
00:22:27,490 --> 00:22:30,130
It creates dependency, not empowerment.

523
00:22:30,150 --> 00:22:33,700
Often it's more about the manager's insecurity than the employee's ability.

524
00:22:34,060 --> 00:22:35,440
The examples are so relatable.

525
00:22:36,080 --> 00:22:39,510
He tries to micromanage by telling everyone exactly how to do their job.

526
00:22:39,670 --> 00:22:40,390
Or that plea.

527
00:22:40,410 --> 00:22:42,730
No one likes to be micromanaged.

528
00:22:43,010 --> 00:22:44,410
Can't you just trust me?

529
00:22:44,430 --> 00:22:48,300
It really shows a breakdown in effective leadership and trust.

530
00:22:48,620 --> 00:22:50,640
Can definitely push good people away.

531
00:22:50,620 --> 00:22:52,160
OK, let's tackle those acronyms.

532
00:22:52,400 --> 00:22:53,740
They're like little codes, aren't they?

533
00:22:54,140 --> 00:22:58,610
But once you know them, they make communication, especially written communication, so much faster.

534
00:22:58,710 --> 00:22:59,350
They really do.

535
00:22:59,410 --> 00:23:04,420
And they might seem like just shortcuts, but you really need to know them to navigate modern business comms.

536
00:23:04,920 --> 00:23:07,230
Misunderstanding them can cause real problems.

537
00:23:07,290 --> 00:23:07,650
For sure.

538
00:23:07,910 --> 00:23:11,170
First one, EOD stands for end of day.

539
00:23:12,280 --> 00:23:13,220
Very common in writing.

540
00:23:13,350 --> 00:23:16,510
Yeah, you mostly see it in emails, maybe project tracking tools.

541
00:23:16,840 --> 00:23:19,420
It's just a super concise way to set a deadline.

542
00:23:19,880 --> 00:23:21,000
How does it help efficiency?

543
00:23:21,300 --> 00:23:22,760
Well, it's unambiguous usually.

544
00:23:22,820 --> 00:23:29,130
EOD means by the close of business on that day, saves typing, makes the deadline crystal clear instantly.

545
00:23:29,360 --> 00:23:31,280
Like, need this report by EOD Wednesday?

546
00:23:31,360 --> 00:23:31,780
Exactly.

547
00:23:31,820 --> 00:23:33,280
You know precisely when it's due.

548
00:23:33,340 --> 00:23:34,820
Very practical, very direct.

549
00:23:35,060 --> 00:23:35,740
Clear and concise.

550
00:23:36,650 --> 00:23:37,130
Okay, next.

551
00:23:38,990 --> 00:23:39,070
Ooh.

552
00:23:39,520 --> 00:23:40,810
Stands for out of office.

553
00:23:41,090 --> 00:23:42,430
The classic auto reply.

554
00:23:42,530 --> 00:23:42,730
Right.

555
00:23:42,960 --> 00:23:48,520
The email bounce back you get when someone's on vacation or at a conference or just unavailable for a while.

556
00:23:49,140 --> 00:23:50,330
What's its practical function?

557
00:23:50,440 --> 00:23:52,660
It's crucial for managing expectations.

558
00:23:52,830 --> 00:23:59,980
For the sender, you immediately know not to expect a quick reply, and it often tells you who else to contact or when the person is back.

559
00:24:00,100 --> 00:24:02,000
Stops you chasing them or getting frustrated.

560
00:24:02,180 --> 00:24:02,640
Exactly.

561
00:24:03,010 --> 00:24:10,440
And for the person who is voodoo, it gives them cover to actually disconnect without worrying about urgent things piling up unaddressed.

562
00:24:10,420 --> 00:24:12,840
Like, sorry, I'll be ooh until Feb 5th.

563
00:24:13,320 --> 00:24:14,930
Contact Jane for urgent matters.

564
00:24:15,130 --> 00:24:15,330
Yep.

565
00:24:16,090 --> 00:24:16,490
Simple.

566
00:24:16,930 --> 00:24:18,010
Universally understood.

567
00:24:18,230 --> 00:24:20,350
Keeps things moving even when people are away.

568
00:24:20,710 --> 00:24:21,310
Super helpful.

569
00:24:21,890 --> 00:24:26,040
Okay, number three, ASAP, or as soon as possible.

570
00:24:26,240 --> 00:24:27,380
So what does this really mean?

571
00:24:27,880 --> 00:24:29,370
Signals big urgency, right?

572
00:24:29,550 --> 00:24:30,630
Used in writing and speaking.

573
00:24:30,730 --> 00:24:31,650
Massive urgency.

574
00:24:31,670 --> 00:24:35,110
You can say it as the word ASAP or spell it out ASAP.

575
00:24:35,090 --> 00:24:38,020
Given how strong it is, when should we actually use it?

576
00:24:38,340 --> 00:24:39,400
Ah, that's the key question.

577
00:24:39,560 --> 00:24:45,850
Because it conveys such intense urgency, like drop everything, do this now, it loses its power if you overuse it.

578
00:24:45,990 --> 00:24:46,950
The cry wolf effect.

579
00:24:47,190 --> 00:24:47,690
Exactly.

580
00:24:47,750 --> 00:24:50,090
If everything is ASAP, then nothing is.

581
00:24:50,150 --> 00:24:51,250
It just stresses people out.

582
00:24:51,670 --> 00:24:57,380
So reserve it for truly critical situations where immediate action is genuinely needed.

583
00:24:57,820 --> 00:25:02,770
Ask yourself, is there a real negative consequence if this doesn't happen right now?

584
00:25:02,830 --> 00:25:03,840
That's a great filter.

585
00:25:04,060 --> 00:25:07,290
If the answer is no, it's probably not ASA, use it wisely.

586
00:25:07,370 --> 00:25:09,590
Like, send those figures ASAP.

587
00:25:09,650 --> 00:25:10,540
The client's waiting.

588
00:25:11,040 --> 00:25:12,040
That feels justified.

589
00:25:12,080 --> 00:25:12,260
Right.

590
00:25:12,380 --> 00:25:13,080
Save its power.

591
00:25:13,560 --> 00:25:14,190
Okay, almost there.

592
00:25:14,690 --> 00:25:17,090
Next is FYI for your information.

593
00:25:17,300 --> 00:25:18,180
Quick heads up.

594
00:25:18,340 --> 00:25:22,330
Yeah, a short way to add extra info, mostly in writing, but you hear it sometimes too.

595
00:25:22,930 --> 00:25:24,050
What's its main job?

596
00:25:24,070 --> 00:25:28,160
Its main job is to share relevant info without necessarily needing a response or action.

597
00:25:28,300 --> 00:25:31,300
It's like saying, thought you should know this, or here's some background.

598
00:25:31,460 --> 00:25:33,900
Kinks people informed without adding to their to-do list.

599
00:25:34,010 --> 00:25:34,460
Exactly.

600
00:25:34,530 --> 00:25:39,610
It prevents future questions sometimes, mostly written like, FYI, the meeting time changed to 1.30.

601
00:25:40,390 --> 00:25:45,040
But yeah, sometimes spoken casually, oh, FYI, can't make the party, efficient info sharing.

602
00:25:45,200 --> 00:25:45,900
Got it.

603
00:25:45,880 --> 00:25:48,390
And finally, ending the week on a happy note.

604
00:25:49,230 --> 00:25:52,660
TGIF means thank God goodness it's Friday.

605
00:25:52,940 --> 00:25:53,700
The weekend cheer.

606
00:25:54,090 --> 00:25:54,470
Totally.

607
00:25:55,210 --> 00:25:57,940
That feeling of relief, excitement for the weekend.

608
00:25:58,480 --> 00:25:59,880
What role does this play at work?

609
00:25:59,940 --> 00:26:01,010
Is it too informal?

610
00:26:01,440 --> 00:26:07,370
Generally, it adds a nice human, informal touch, especially in team chats or quick sign offs on a Friday.

611
00:26:07,410 --> 00:26:10,670
It builds camaraderie, acknowledges that shared relief.

612
00:26:10,990 --> 00:26:12,370
Texts people over the coming weekend.

613
00:26:12,470 --> 00:26:12,670
Yeah.

614
00:26:12,690 --> 00:26:16,040
While it is informal, it's so common and understood.

615
00:26:16,100 --> 00:26:20,980
It's usually fine in less formal internal comms like great work this week, team.

616
00:26:21,140 --> 00:26:22,850
TGIF, have a good weekend.

617
00:26:23,230 --> 00:26:24,410
Humanizes things a bit.

618
00:26:24,590 --> 00:26:25,930
Hashtag tag tag outro.

619
00:26:25,910 --> 00:26:26,230
Wow.

620
00:26:26,290 --> 00:26:26,490
Okay.

621
00:26:26,510 --> 00:26:27,390
That was a lot.

622
00:26:27,410 --> 00:26:28,820
We've really covered some ground today.

623
00:26:29,130 --> 00:26:37,050
From those polite starters like when you have a minute to strategic terms like scalable, that difference between a slacker and a workhorse.

624
00:26:37,310 --> 00:26:37,850
Big difference.

625
00:26:38,070 --> 00:26:38,530
Huge.

626
00:26:38,950 --> 00:26:44,760
And then all those handy acronyms, EOD, OOASAP, FYI, TGIF.

627
00:26:44,740 --> 00:26:49,350
You listening should now have a really solid toolkit of essential business English.

