00:30:18,880 --> 00:30:19,080
All right.

688
00:30:19,100 --> 00:30:21,180
Let's kick things off with that's more than a phrase.

689
00:30:21,240 --> 00:30:25,370
It's almost a feeling, something I think pretty much everyone can relate to, especially after a nice weekend.

690
00:30:25,390 --> 00:30:29,430
It's kind of a two for one, a case of the Mondays or just Mondays.

691
00:30:29,650 --> 00:30:29,970
Am I right?

692
00:30:30,290 --> 00:30:30,410
Yeah.

693
00:30:30,830 --> 00:30:31,360
Now, be honest.

694
00:30:31,710 --> 00:30:36,000
Do you leave out of bed at 6 a.m. Monday morning just buzzing with excitement for the work week?

695
00:30:35,980 --> 00:30:37,120
Yeah, probably not, right?

696
00:30:37,740 --> 00:30:47,890
I mean, if you do, good for you, but for most of us, there's that little bit of drag, that slight lack of enthusiasm maybe.

697
00:30:47,930 --> 00:30:50,680
It's a pretty universal feeling after a weekend off.

698
00:30:51,180 --> 00:30:57,250
So when someone says, oh, I totally got a case of the Mondays, or they sigh and go, Mondays, am I right?

699
00:30:57,230 --> 00:31:05,680
They're using this lighthearted way to blame Monday for feeling unmotivated, maybe a bit grumpy, or even for stuff that's completely unrelated, like the printer jamming again.

700
00:31:05,940 --> 00:31:08,540
You might hear, ah, I just can't get motivated for this spreadsheet.

701
00:31:08,560 --> 00:31:09,680
Definitely a case of the Mondays.

702
00:31:10,160 --> 00:31:14,250
Or if the Wi-Fi dies mid-presentation, someone might mutter, of course, Monday's right.

703
00:31:14,570 --> 00:31:18,400
It's obviously not Monday's fault the Internet's down, but it's this funny shared complaint.

704
00:31:18,960 --> 00:31:20,720
Poor Monday gets blamed for everything.

705
00:31:20,700 --> 00:31:21,380
It really does.

706
00:31:21,520 --> 00:31:26,150
And what's fascinating here, like you said, it builds camaraderie, but it also works on a deeper level.

707
00:31:26,210 --> 00:31:30,660
It's almost like a psychological release valve in the workplace.

708
00:31:30,900 --> 00:31:31,260
Think about it.

709
00:31:31,280 --> 00:31:34,760
Professional culture often expects us to be positive, you know, can do.

710
00:31:35,160 --> 00:31:39,130
So Mondays becomes this sort of socially acceptable scapegoat.

711
00:31:39,110 --> 00:31:44,360
You can voice a minor shared frustration without sounding negative or complaining about your actual work.

712
00:31:44,440 --> 00:31:50,290
It's a way to bond a little bit of we're all in this together grumbling that doesn't really criticize anything specific.

713
00:31:50,350 --> 00:31:55,960
It acknowledges that shared feeling that transition back to work without making a big deal out of it.

714
00:31:56,250 --> 00:31:57,390
Kind of clever, really.

715
00:31:57,370 --> 00:31:59,130
A psychological release valve.

716
00:31:59,170 --> 00:31:59,510
I like that.

717
00:31:59,530 --> 00:32:00,320
That's a great way to put it.

718
00:32:00,540 --> 00:32:01,140
OK, moving on.

719
00:32:02,340 --> 00:32:05,930
Our next phrase is all about politeness, about respect.

720
00:32:06,530 --> 00:32:07,130
Crucial stuff.

721
00:32:07,660 --> 00:32:10,040
It's when you have a minute or the very similar.

722
00:32:10,080 --> 00:32:12,390
Do you have a minute?

723
00:32:12,370 --> 00:32:17,460
This is just such a useful, gentle way to ask for someone's time or help.

724
00:32:17,480 --> 00:32:19,030
You're not interrupting them forcefully.

725
00:32:19,050 --> 00:32:20,710
You're not demanding their attention right now.

726
00:32:21,390 --> 00:32:22,430
It shows consideration.

727
00:32:22,470 --> 00:32:26,920
So you could say, yeah, when you have a minute, could you glance over this report before I send it?

728
00:32:27,020 --> 00:32:30,110
Or slightly more direct, do you have a minute to look at this client email?

729
00:32:30,090 --> 00:32:33,780
It's perfect for when you see a colleague is clearly focused.

730
00:32:34,060 --> 00:32:39,120
Maybe they're really in the zone, but you need their input on something that isn't like code red urgent.

731
00:32:39,380 --> 00:32:40,940
You're basically saying, I see you're busy.

732
00:32:40,980 --> 00:32:41,700
Respect that.

733
00:32:41,970 --> 00:32:44,230
But when you have a moment, could you help with this small thing?

734
00:32:44,320 --> 00:32:48,420
And if we connect this to the bigger picture, it's more than just politeness.

735
00:32:48,880 --> 00:32:51,940
It's really fundamental to building good professional relationships.

736
00:32:52,360 --> 00:32:54,950
It shows you respect their time, their workload.

737
00:32:55,270 --> 00:32:58,330
It's an implicit acknowledgement that their focus is valuable.

738
00:32:58,350 --> 00:33:00,130
You're not demanding, you're requesting.

739
00:33:00,790 --> 00:33:03,880
And crucially, you're giving them control over when they respond.

740
00:33:03,980 --> 00:33:07,420
Contrast that with just walking up and saying, look at this now.

741
00:33:07,400 --> 00:33:10,040
The difference in tone in the dynamic is huge.

742
00:33:10,240 --> 00:33:12,370
One's a command, the other's a collaboration.

743
00:33:12,810 --> 00:33:15,930
That collaborative feel is what you want in a healthy, productive team.

744
00:33:15,970 --> 00:33:16,760
It builds goodwill.

745
00:33:16,860 --> 00:33:17,460
It really does.

746
00:33:18,080 --> 00:33:19,760
From command to collaborative query.

747
00:33:21,080 --> 00:33:22,300
Yeah, that sums it up perfectly.

748
00:33:22,980 --> 00:33:24,230
It signals cooperation.

749
00:33:25,170 --> 00:33:29,150
Okay, building on that collaborative idea, phrase number three is a fun one.

750
00:33:29,670 --> 00:33:31,300
Bounce ideas off of...

751
00:33:31,780 --> 00:33:33,270
I always picture like a bouncy ball.

752
00:33:33,350 --> 00:33:40,610
You have an idea, maybe just the start of one, you toss it to someone else, they catch it, maybe add a little spin, their own perspective and bounce it back.

753
00:33:41,330 --> 00:33:44,960
And each time it bounces, the idea gets a bit better, a bit more refined.

754
00:33:45,760 --> 00:33:53,770
So bounce ideas off of you means you wanna share a thought, maybe it's not fully formed yet and get someone's reaction, their gut feeling, their initial take.

755
00:33:54,490 --> 00:33:58,630
The key is you're usually not looking for final answers or a big commitment at this stage.

756
00:33:58,670 --> 00:34:00,690
It's more about exploring, refining,

757
00:34:00,670 --> 00:34:02,800
You might say, hey, I'm wrestling with this marketing angle.

758
00:34:02,880 --> 00:34:03,820
Got five minutes later.

759
00:34:04,140 --> 00:34:05,860
I'd love to bounce some initial thoughts off of you.

760
00:34:05,940 --> 00:34:08,200
You're asking for their input, not a finished plan.

761
00:34:08,610 --> 00:34:11,330
Or maybe I've been thinking about the project kickoff.

762
00:34:11,650 --> 00:34:14,700
And before I write anything formal, can I bounce a few ideas off you?

763
00:34:14,740 --> 00:34:15,840
See if I'm heading the right way.

764
00:34:16,600 --> 00:34:20,880
And just a quick note, you'll often hear people drop the F. So bounce ideas off you.

765
00:34:21,180 --> 00:34:22,130
Both are totally fine.

766
00:34:22,330 --> 00:34:23,090
Totally common.

767
00:34:23,150 --> 00:34:27,260
And this raises such an important point about how work gets done now.

768
00:34:27,480 --> 00:34:34,930
It signals that you want constructive feedback, that you see value in co-creation, not just getting a rubber stamp on your own idea.

769
00:34:34,970 --> 00:34:39,720
It implies that ideas get better when people collaborate, when different perspectives come in early.

770
00:34:40,020 --> 00:34:44,710
It encourages sharing things that aren't perfect yet, which is really vital for innovation.

771
00:34:44,750 --> 00:34:47,670
You're inviting someone to be part of the shaping process.

772
00:34:47,650 --> 00:34:54,070
It taps into that collective intelligence, helps spot problems early, avoids going too far down a path that maybe isn't the best one.

773
00:34:54,150 --> 00:34:56,850
It's a smart, proactive way to develop ideas.

774
00:34:57,210 --> 00:34:57,660
Absolutely.

775
00:34:57,780 --> 00:35:01,380
The co-creation collective intelligence is much more than just a quick chat.

776
00:35:01,980 --> 00:35:02,990
Okay, number four.

777
00:35:03,570 --> 00:35:05,250
This one's about timing, about commitment.

778
00:35:05,630 --> 00:35:06,470
First thing in the morning.

779
00:35:06,710 --> 00:35:07,220
Mm-hmm.

780
00:35:07,200 --> 00:35:10,640
Now, linguistically, notice there's no the here.

781
00:35:10,920 --> 00:35:13,030
It's first thing in morning.

782
00:35:13,050 --> 00:35:17,150
Small detail, but it's the fixed phrase, a common slip up for learners.

783
00:35:17,710 --> 00:35:19,000
And the meaning is super clear.

784
00:35:20,280 --> 00:35:23,160
It means right away at the very start of the next workday.

785
00:35:23,560 --> 00:35:24,050
No delay.

786
00:35:24,390 --> 00:35:26,150
It's a promise of tromped action.

787
00:35:26,130 --> 00:35:28,780
Like personally, first thing in the morning, I always make coffee.

788
00:35:28,840 --> 00:35:32,780
Professionally, you might say, sorry, I didn't quite finish the report today, but I'll do it first thing in the morning.

789
00:35:32,890 --> 00:35:34,110
It's not just sometime tomorrow.

790
00:35:34,550 --> 00:35:35,310
It's specific.

791
00:35:35,330 --> 00:35:36,800
It sets a clear expectation.

792
00:35:37,180 --> 00:35:39,100
Everyone knows when that task is hitting the top of the list.

793
00:35:39,320 --> 00:35:44,270
And what's fascinating, host, is how much commitment is packed into that simple phrase.

794
00:35:44,250 --> 00:35:45,880
It signals priority.

795
00:35:45,920 --> 00:35:47,260
It signals reliability.

796
00:35:47,580 --> 00:35:50,790
In busy workplaces with tight deadlines, that clarity is gold.

797
00:35:51,130 --> 00:35:52,550
It removes ambiguity.

798
00:35:52,710 --> 00:35:54,710
Your colleagues, your boss, they know when to expect it.

799
00:35:54,830 --> 00:35:56,300
They can plan their own work around that.

800
00:35:56,360 --> 00:35:58,920
It helps manage deadlines effectively, ensures accountability.

801
00:35:58,960 --> 00:36:02,110
It's a small phrase with a big impact on workflow and predictability.

802
00:36:02,410 --> 00:36:03,210
It's exactly that.

803
00:36:03,250 --> 00:36:04,810
Removal of ambiguity is key.

804
00:36:05,260 --> 00:36:06,500
Helps everyone stay coordinated.

805
00:36:07,710 --> 00:36:09,290
OK, now we circle back.

806
00:36:09,990 --> 00:36:14,220
Back to our initial Frankenstein moment for phrase number five, pick your brains.

807
00:36:15,100 --> 00:36:16,470
Let's clear this one up tentatively.

808
00:36:16,800 --> 00:36:19,070
No actual brain picking involved, thankfully.

809
00:36:19,530 --> 00:36:23,240
It simply means you want to ask for advice, for input, for insights from someone who

810
00:36:23,220 --> 00:36:27,360
knows more about a topic than you do, someone with experience, expertise.

811
00:36:27,700 --> 00:36:31,010
It's actually a really respectful way to tap into their knowledge.

812
00:36:31,030 --> 00:36:32,810
You're acknowledging they have valuable insights.

813
00:36:33,290 --> 00:36:39,570
So if you're new somewhere, you might say to a long-timer, hey, since you've been here five years, could I pick your brain sometime of the company culture?

814
00:36:39,950 --> 00:36:44,120
You're asking for their perspective, their wisdom, or maybe before a big meeting.

815
00:36:44,140 --> 00:36:45,180
Could we grab 15 minutes?

816
00:36:45,240 --> 00:36:48,580
I'd love to pick your brains about your thoughts for the next quarter's strategy.

817
00:36:48,560 --> 00:36:50,180
It shows you value their opinion.

818
00:36:50,220 --> 00:36:52,670
You're seeking informed insight, not just chitchat.

819
00:36:52,950 --> 00:36:53,910
It's a compliment, really.

820
00:36:54,210 --> 00:36:54,730
Absolutely.

821
00:36:54,830 --> 00:37:05,350
And if we connect this to the bigger picture of how organizations learn and grow, this phrase really highlights the value placed on experience, on mentorship, on that internal knowledge base.

822
00:37:05,950 --> 00:37:11,260
When you ask to pick someone's brains, you're showing deference, yes, but also a genuine desire to learn from them.

823
00:37:11,280 --> 00:37:12,920
You're acknowledging their expertise.

824
00:37:12,900 --> 00:37:18,120
In a good company culture, this fosters knowledge sharing, helps newer people get up to speed faster.

825
00:37:18,180 --> 00:37:22,890
It's crucial for development, both for the individual asking and for the organization as a whole.

826
00:37:23,250 --> 00:37:26,870
It keeps that valuable, often unwritten, knowledge circulating.

827
00:37:27,290 --> 00:37:29,640
Yeah, seeing it as a compliment totally reframes it.

828
00:37:31,480 --> 00:37:36,960
Okay, and speaking of asking for time, that leads nicely into phrase number six, to hop on a call.

829
00:37:38,430 --> 00:37:39,710
This is everywhere now, isn't it?

830
00:37:40,050 --> 00:37:41,710
Especially with remote and hybrid work.

831
00:37:42,190 --> 00:37:47,440
And yeah, it can mean an actual phone call, but let's be honest, more often than not these days it means a video call.

832
00:37:47,720 --> 00:37:49,990
Zoom, Teams, Google Meet, you name it.

833
00:37:50,950 --> 00:37:59,240
The key thing about hopping on a call is usually the implication that it's fairly quick, maybe a bit spontaneous, not a big formally scheduled meeting with an agenda,

834
00:37:59,220 --> 00:38:02,570
As for a targeted discussion, you might hear, hey, do you have a minute to hop on a call?

835
00:38:02,870 --> 00:38:04,450
The client just asked something unexpected.

836
00:38:04,910 --> 00:38:09,000
It implies immediacy, a need to talk it through quickly, or maybe.

837
00:38:09,020 --> 00:38:11,670
I was going to email you about this, but it might be easier to just talk.

838
00:38:11,730 --> 00:38:13,310
Can we hop on a call for 10 minutes?

839
00:38:13,330 --> 00:38:15,660
It suggests talking is more efficient than writing it all out.

840
00:38:15,640 --> 00:38:19,220
And this raises a really important point about communication efficiency.

841
00:38:19,720 --> 00:38:24,930
Sometimes wrestling with a complex issue over email just creates more confusion, takes way longer.

842
00:38:24,970 --> 00:38:27,040
A quick verbal chat can cut through that.

843
00:38:27,460 --> 00:38:29,720
Clarify things instantly, make a decision.

844
00:38:29,940 --> 00:38:31,390
This phrase, hop on a call,

845
00:38:31,590 --> 00:38:34,390
facilitates that direct real-time problem solving.

846
00:38:34,650 --> 00:38:39,190
It's crucial in fast-moving environments where you need quick answers, quick feedback to keep things rolling.

847
00:38:39,550 --> 00:38:42,720
It's about choosing the right tool for the job, communication-wise.

848
00:38:43,040 --> 00:38:47,100
And sometimes that tool is just talking to someone directly, even if it's through a screen.

849
00:38:47,580 --> 00:38:47,980
So true.

850
00:38:48,520 --> 00:38:50,120
Saves so much time sometimes.

851
00:38:50,780 --> 00:38:53,010
Okay, Freight 7 brings another sense of speed.

852
00:38:53,730 --> 00:38:57,070
Shoot off an email or fire off an email.

853
00:38:57,220 --> 00:39:04,010
the verbs here, shoot, fire, they immediately give you that feeling of quickness, efficiency, maybe even a little bit of urgency.

854
00:39:04,350 --> 00:39:08,300
So it means sending an email quickly, efficiently, getting it done and out the door.

855
00:39:08,700 --> 00:39:10,800
And it's not just email, you could shoot off a text too.

856
00:39:11,300 --> 00:39:11,800
Same idea.

857
00:39:12,360 --> 00:39:13,780
Swift, organized communication.

858
00:39:14,040 --> 00:39:17,610
You might say, okay, I'm gonna fire off an email with these questions before the client meeting.

859
00:39:17,630 --> 00:39:23,100
Shows you're being proactive, getting things sorted quickly, or remember first thing in the morning, you could combine them.

860
00:39:23,320 --> 00:39:26,380
Shoot me an email with the details and I'll look at it first thing in the morning.

861
00:39:26,360 --> 00:39:27,500
See how they work together.

862
00:39:28,000 --> 00:39:29,830
Clear, fast, action-oriented.

863
00:39:30,030 --> 00:39:34,710
What's fascinating here is that implied sense of action and organization.

864
00:39:35,130 --> 00:39:36,500
It's not just sending an email.

865
00:39:36,560 --> 00:39:38,000
It's sending it purposefully, promptly.

866
00:39:38,040 --> 00:39:39,440
It suggests you're on top of things.

867
00:39:39,460 --> 00:39:40,460
Your workflow is smooth.

868
00:39:40,800 --> 00:39:43,030
It also helps manage expectations about response times.

869
00:39:43,470 --> 00:39:45,830
In business today, speed is often valued.

870
00:39:46,190 --> 00:39:49,780
Saying you'll shoot off an email implies you'll do it without unnecessary delay.

871
00:39:49,800 --> 00:39:52,000
It contributes to that culture of responsiveness.

872
00:39:51,980 --> 00:39:57,450
Yeah, these little phrases carry so much unspoken meaning about efficiency and intent.

873
00:39:58,170 --> 00:40:01,650
Okay, number eight is about making sure everyone's informed.

874
00:40:02,150 --> 00:40:03,300
Keep someone in the loop.

875
00:40:04,140 --> 00:40:05,240
Picture a loop, like a circle.

876
00:40:05,520 --> 00:40:07,500
If you're in the loop, you're connected, you know what's going on.

877
00:40:07,920 --> 00:40:09,910
If you're out of the loop, you're missing information.

878
00:40:10,910 --> 00:40:12,430
The goal is to keep the right people in.

879
00:40:12,930 --> 00:40:19,420
So this phrase means making sure someone is continuously updated about developments, discussions, progress.

880
00:40:19,400 --> 00:40:20,720
Keeping them informed.

881
00:40:21,160 --> 00:40:25,030
For instance, if you missed a meeting, you might say, hey, sorry, I couldn't make the meeting yesterday.

882
00:40:25,370 --> 00:40:26,910
Could you keep me in the loop on the decisions?

883
00:40:27,110 --> 00:40:28,290
Maybe forward the key emails.

884
00:40:28,790 --> 00:40:30,660
You want to be brought back into the loop.

885
00:40:30,680 --> 00:40:31,880
And it's flexible with pronouns.

886
00:40:31,900 --> 00:40:34,840
You can say, the boss wants you to keep her in the loop on this project.

887
00:40:35,380 --> 00:40:37,140
That means regular updates are expected.

888
00:40:37,760 --> 00:40:38,990
She wants to know everything relevant.

889
00:40:39,330 --> 00:40:40,090
Keep him in the loop.

890
00:40:40,190 --> 00:40:40,850
Keep him in the loop.

891
00:40:41,270 --> 00:40:41,930
Very useful.

892
00:40:41,910 --> 00:40:46,700
And connecting this to teamwork and project management, this phrase is absolutely fundamental.

893
00:40:47,060 --> 00:40:53,430
It's all about transparency, shared understanding, and avoiding mistakes that happen when people don't have the information they need.

894
00:40:53,970 --> 00:41:02,500
When everyone who needs to know is in the loop, decisions are better, collaboration is smoother, problems get spotted earlier, it prevents silos, ensures alignment.

895
00:41:02,520 --> 00:41:05,560
It's crucial for making sure projects run successfully and efficiently.

896
00:41:06,020 --> 00:41:11,010
And that ability to specify who needs to be looped in her, him, them, the marketing team,

897
00:41:11,360 --> 00:41:12,620
makes it really precise.

898
00:41:12,720 --> 00:41:15,350
It builds that shared awareness critical for success.

899
00:41:15,630 --> 00:41:17,220
Transparency and shared understanding.

900
00:41:17,420 --> 00:41:18,040
Couldn't agree more.

901
00:41:18,620 --> 00:41:20,890
The foundation of good teamwork.

902
00:41:20,910 --> 00:41:22,170
Okay, number nine is a big one.

903
00:41:22,330 --> 00:41:22,950
Very common.

904
00:41:23,560 --> 00:41:24,440
Brainstorm.

905
00:41:25,210 --> 00:41:27,890
This is all about generating new ideas, usually in a group setting.

906
00:41:28,420 --> 00:41:31,180
The whole point of brainstorming is to just throw ideas out there.

907
00:41:31,220 --> 00:41:34,040
Good ones, bad ones, crazy ones, doesn't matter at the start.

908
00:41:34,060 --> 00:41:38,910
You just want to get a volume of ideas down, see what sparks, what connections happen.

909
00:41:39,430 --> 00:41:41,710
Quantity over quality initially.

910
00:41:41,730 --> 00:41:47,580
So you might hear, okay team, tomorrow morning, 10 a.m., we're going to brainstorm new marketing angles for the Q4 campaign.

911
00:41:48,160 --> 00:41:49,900
The aim is just generating possibilities.

912
00:41:49,880 --> 00:41:54,430
Or we had a session last week to brainstorm ways to improve customer onboarding.

913
00:41:54,650 --> 00:41:59,420
It's about collective creativity, tapping into everyone's thoughts to solve a problem or create something new.

914
00:41:59,640 --> 00:42:03,390
And this raises that vital point about collective intelligence again, doesn't it?

915
00:42:03,800 --> 00:42:09,170
Brainstorming, done well, unlocks ideas that one person alone might never think of.

916
00:42:09,610 --> 00:42:11,400
It brings diverse perspectives together.

917
00:42:11,480 --> 00:42:14,320
A key part of it is creating that safe space.

918
00:42:14,300 --> 00:42:15,480
No judgment initially.

919
00:42:15,600 --> 00:42:16,750
Just get the ideas out.

920
00:42:16,790 --> 00:42:23,700
That's crucial for encouraging people to share maybe half-formed thoughts or unconventional suggestions that could actually lead to a breakthrough.

921
00:42:24,120 --> 00:42:26,400
It challenges assumptions, opens up new avenues.

922
00:42:26,460 --> 00:42:31,870
It's a really powerful tool for innovation and problem solving when you harness that group energy effectively.

923
00:42:31,920 --> 00:42:33,100
Definitely an unlocks potential.

924
00:42:33,940 --> 00:42:36,810
Okay, rounding out this section, number 10 is debrief.

925
00:42:36,830 --> 00:42:38,770
This is about what happens after something is done.

926
00:42:38,930 --> 00:42:46,620
To debrief means to summarize, to discuss, to share information after an event, a project, a meeting, even a sales pitch.

927
00:42:46,640 --> 00:42:48,640
It's a look back, a structured reflection.

928
00:42:48,620 --> 00:42:52,450
So a project manager might say, OK, the project is officially wrapped.

929
00:42:52,850 --> 00:42:55,350
Let's schedule a meeting next week to debrief.

930
00:42:55,370 --> 00:42:58,260
In that meeting, you talk about what went well, what didn't, what you learned.

931
00:42:58,620 --> 00:43:00,020
The goal is continuous improvement.

932
00:43:00,140 --> 00:43:02,950
How can we do better next time based on this experience?

933
00:43:03,350 --> 00:43:04,550
You can use it as a noun, too.

934
00:43:05,110 --> 00:43:07,330
We had a really productive debrief after the conference.

935
00:43:07,810 --> 00:43:09,780
It refers to the meeting itself.

936
00:43:09,800 --> 00:43:10,380
Same function.

937
00:43:10,900 --> 00:43:12,120
Analyze, learn, improve.

938
00:43:12,200 --> 00:43:17,040
What's really fascinating here is how critical debriefing is for learning and improvement.

939
00:43:17,610 --> 00:43:18,650
It's not just a chat.

940
00:43:18,810 --> 00:43:20,310
It's a structured process.

941
00:43:20,630 --> 00:43:25,110
You systematically unpack what happened, the successes, the failures, the unexpected hurdles.

942
00:43:25,630 --> 00:43:27,270
This prevents repeating mistakes.

943
00:43:27,650 --> 00:43:29,180
It helps capture best practices.

944
00:43:29,530 --> 00:43:32,440
It shares lessons learned across the team or even the organization.

945
00:43:32,940 --> 00:43:40,700
That distinction between to debrief the action and a debrief, the event, shows it's a recognized formal part of many workflows.

946
00:43:40,680 --> 00:43:42,970
It signals a commitment to getting better over time.

947
00:43:43,090 --> 00:43:43,270
Right.

948
00:43:43,710 --> 00:43:44,310
Great foundation.

949
00:43:44,970 --> 00:43:48,380
Now that we've covered those daily interaction phrases, let's shift gears a bit in part two.

950
00:43:48,780 --> 00:43:54,690
We're going to look at phrases that often describe people or workplace dynamics, sometimes more strategic stuff.

951
00:43:55,290 --> 00:43:59,640
And let's start with one you really, really hope isn't used about you.

952
00:44:00,300 --> 00:44:00,900
Phrase 11.

953
00:44:02,420 --> 00:44:02,980
Slacker.

954
00:44:03,880 --> 00:44:04,780
Oof.

955
00:44:04,800 --> 00:44:05,640
Nobody wants that label.

956
00:44:06,280 --> 00:44:08,290
A slacker is basically someone seen as lazy.

957
00:44:08,870 --> 00:44:11,010
Someone who doesn't pull their weight, doesn't do their fair share.

958
00:44:11,670 --> 00:44:13,560
It's a pretty harsh judgment in a work context.

959
00:44:14,000 --> 00:44:19,260
You might hear someone complaining, maybe discreetly, oh, he's such a slacker, always late with his parts, and I end up having to pick up the slack.

960
00:44:19,930 --> 00:44:21,630
It implies frustration, unfairness.

961
00:44:22,310 --> 00:44:23,670
And think about group projects, right?

962
00:44:24,010 --> 00:44:24,810
School or work.

963
00:44:25,150 --> 00:44:29,220
Seems like every group has at least one slacker who just coasts while everyone else does the work.

964
00:44:29,240 --> 00:44:31,160
It creates resentment, drags the whole team down.

965
00:44:31,220 --> 00:44:32,340
It's a really negative term.

966
00:44:32,320 --> 00:44:36,390
And what's fascinating, Host, is that strong negative judgment baked in.

967
00:44:36,570 --> 00:44:37,950
It hits on core issues.

968
00:44:38,710 --> 00:44:43,700
Teamwork, fairness, accountability, that unspoken agreement that everyone contributes.

969
00:44:44,180 --> 00:44:46,430
Having a slacker on the team can just kill morale.

970
00:44:46,470 --> 00:44:48,370
People feel resentful, taken advantage of.

971
00:44:48,410 --> 00:44:50,270
Productivity obviously suffers.

972
00:44:50,610 --> 00:44:52,580
It's a breach of that team contract.

973
00:44:53,240 --> 00:44:55,340
Understanding the weight of this term is important.

974
00:44:55,420 --> 00:44:58,030
It signals a serious problem in team dynamics.

975
00:44:58,010 --> 00:44:59,990
Yeah, definitely a breach of contract.

976
00:45:00,010 --> 00:45:03,610
Now, the complete opposite and a much nicer label to have is phrase 12.

977
00:45:04,250 --> 00:45:05,130
Workhorse.

978
00:45:05,150 --> 00:45:05,950
This is a positive one.

979
00:45:06,110 --> 00:45:10,020
A workhorse is someone who works really hard, consistently, diligently.

980
00:45:10,320 --> 00:45:11,220
They just get stuff done.

981
00:45:11,500 --> 00:45:13,440
It's a huge compliment in most workplaces.

982
00:45:13,720 --> 00:45:21,110
You might hear she's a real workhorse, always reliable, always delivering high quality work, often goes the extra mile or maybe advocating for someone.

983
00:45:21,090 --> 00:45:22,530
He really deserves that promotion.

984
00:45:22,660 --> 00:45:23,700
Total workhorse.

985
00:45:23,720 --> 00:45:24,780
Invaluable to the team.

986
00:45:25,000 --> 00:45:27,970
It implies reliability, dedication, strong work ethic.

987
00:45:27,990 --> 00:45:29,310
Someone you can absolutely count on.

988
00:45:29,750 --> 00:45:32,820
Hopefully, you listening are seen more as a workhorse than a slacker.

989
00:45:33,070 --> 00:45:39,000
If we connect this to career paths, workhorse signifies those qualities that organizations really value.

990
00:45:39,220 --> 00:45:42,940
Reliability, dedication, strong ethic, consistency.

991
00:45:43,420 --> 00:45:45,850
It implies dependability as someone who follows through.

992
00:45:45,870 --> 00:45:49,450
These are often the people who get recognized, get more responsibility, get promoted.

993
00:45:49,470 --> 00:45:50,410
It builds a reputation.

994
00:45:51,190 --> 00:45:53,050
So it's worth thinking about, right?

995
00:45:53,240 --> 00:45:54,280
How are you perceived?

996
00:45:54,420 --> 00:45:56,500
Are you demonstrating those workhorse qualities?

997
00:45:56,540 --> 00:45:58,220
It can definitely influence your trajectory.

998
00:45:58,240 --> 00:45:59,280
It's a badge of honor.

999
00:45:59,330 --> 00:46:00,790
Absolutely building that reputation.

1000
00:46:01,370 --> 00:46:03,240
Okay, phrase 13, all hands on deck.

1001
00:46:03,940 --> 00:46:09,650
This one has cool origins, literally from sailing ships, needing every sailor on deck for an emergency.

1002
00:46:10,630 --> 00:46:13,070
In business, it's figurative, but the meaning's similar.

1003
00:46:13,510 --> 00:46:22,490
It means everyone needs to pitch in, get involved, contribute to something right now, usually because it's urgent, or there's a huge task that needs a collective effort, a critical moment.

1004
00:46:22,470 --> 00:46:25,390
You'd hear a manager say, OK, team, deadline's looming.

1005
00:46:25,530 --> 00:46:28,910
We need all hands on deck to get this proposal finished by tomorrow.

1006
00:46:29,290 --> 00:46:30,910
It means drop what you're doing.

1007
00:46:31,240 --> 00:46:32,120
This is the priority.

1008
00:46:32,880 --> 00:46:35,040
You might also hear an all-hands meeting.

1009
00:46:35,420 --> 00:46:38,520
That means everyone in the company or department has to attend.

1010
00:46:38,860 --> 00:46:41,290
The CEO called an all-hands meeting for Thursday.

1011
00:46:41,830 --> 00:46:43,630
Signals something important is being announced.

1012
00:46:43,660 --> 00:46:48,190
And this raises that interesting point about mobilizing a team, managing crises.

1013
00:46:48,950 --> 00:46:51,470
All hands on deck is a powerful call to action.

1014
00:46:51,570 --> 00:46:54,540
It signals urgency, signals that this requires everyone's focus.

1015
00:46:54,880 --> 00:46:58,120
It unites the team towards one critical, immediate goal.

1016
00:46:58,480 --> 00:47:01,300
It overrides individual priorities for the collective good.

1017
00:47:01,320 --> 00:47:08,140
It can be really motivating, actually, fostering that sense of shared purpose, shared responsibility in tackling something big or urgent together.

1018
00:47:08,180 --> 00:47:09,060
Yeah.

1019
00:47:09,080 --> 00:47:11,440
When used right, it can really value people.

1020
00:47:11,420 --> 00:47:13,000
Now, for something less motivating.

1021
00:47:13,790 --> 00:47:16,670
Phrase 14, often describing a management style.

1022
00:47:16,690 --> 00:47:17,580
Micromanage.

1023
00:47:18,260 --> 00:47:19,140
Ah, the worst.

1024
00:47:19,840 --> 00:47:26,760
To micromanage is when someone, usually a boss, tries to control every tiny detail of how their employees do their work.

1025
00:47:26,740 --> 00:47:29,260
It's often incredibly frustrating for the person being managed.

1026
00:47:29,810 --> 00:47:31,050
If you've experienced it, you know.

1027
00:47:31,650 --> 00:47:36,740
Someone looking over your shoulder, questioning every decision, telling you exactly how to format an email, it's stifling.

1028
00:47:37,120 --> 00:47:40,080
You might hear someone complain, my boss tries to micromanage everything.

1029
00:47:40,400 --> 00:47:42,980
He tells us how to do our jobs, not just what needs to be done.

1030
00:47:43,470 --> 00:47:46,930
Or you might feel like saying, seriously, no one likes being micromanaged.

1031
00:47:47,110 --> 00:47:48,630
Just trust me to do the work.

1032
00:47:49,360 --> 00:47:51,340
It signals a real lack of trust and autonomy.

1033
00:47:51,540 --> 00:47:55,140
What's fascinating here, host, is just how damaging micromanagement is.

1034
00:47:55,600 --> 00:47:59,870
To morale, yes, but also to autonomy, initiative, even productivity.

1035
00:48:00,210 --> 00:48:01,430
It screams, I don't trust you.

1036
00:48:01,530 --> 00:48:02,550
It kills creativity.

1037
00:48:02,890 --> 00:48:07,140
Why bother suggesting a better way if your boss is just going to dictate every step anyway?

1038
00:48:07,220 --> 00:48:09,820
It leads to disengagement, higher turnover, people just checked out.

1039
00:48:10,180 --> 00:48:11,580
It's fundamentally counterproductive.

1040
00:48:11,600 --> 00:48:14,790
It emphasizes the need for empowerment, for trust, not control.

1041
00:48:14,770 --> 00:48:15,610
Total morale killer.

1042
00:48:15,750 --> 00:48:16,010
Agreed.

1043
00:48:16,890 --> 00:48:18,190
And probably inefficient, too.

1044
00:48:19,060 --> 00:48:20,520
OK, number 15 is align.

1045
00:48:21,160 --> 00:48:23,000
Remember the silent G here, align.

1046
00:48:23,680 --> 00:48:28,010
It means being in agreement, consistent with or supporting something else.

1047
00:48:28,410 --> 00:48:33,520
So strategically, you might say this project doesn't really align with our company's core goals.

1048
00:48:34,000 --> 00:48:34,980
So we're not going forward with it.

1049
00:48:35,390 --> 00:48:36,410
It means there's a mismatch.

1050
00:48:36,870 --> 00:48:38,430
It doesn't fit the bigger picture.

1051
00:48:38,410 --> 00:48:47,150
I get contacted sometimes by companies wanting me to promote things, but if their product doesn't align with my goal of teaching English effectively, I have to say, sorry, our goals don't align.

1052
00:48:47,230 --> 00:48:47,910
It's not a fit.

1053
00:48:48,750 --> 00:48:49,760
It can also mean partnership.

1054
00:48:49,950 --> 00:48:52,720
Our company aligns itself with charities that have similar goals.

1055
00:48:52,860 --> 00:48:53,900
It means they support each other.

1056
00:48:54,140 --> 00:48:56,790
They're on the same page, working towards a shared purpose.

1057
00:48:56,860 --> 00:49:02,140
And connecting that to strategy align is crucial for decision making, for partnerships.

1058
00:49:02,300 --> 00:49:03,510
It's about ensuring coherence.

1059
00:49:03,830 --> 00:49:06,210
Do internal projects align with overall strategy?

1060
00:49:06,470 --> 00:49:08,610
Do potential partners align with our values?

1061
00:49:08,880 --> 00:49:12,300
It helps justify decisions, allocate resources effectively.

1062
00:49:12,580 --> 00:49:16,090
It ensures efforts aren't wasted on things that don't contribute to the main objectives.

1063
00:49:16,550 --> 00:49:19,950
And for partnerships, that alignment of values or goals is fundamental for success.

1064
00:49:20,030 --> 00:49:22,240
Knowing when things align or don't is key.

1065
00:49:22,250 --> 00:49:23,110
Makes total sense.

1066
00:49:23,530 --> 00:49:26,860
It's about strategic fit, avoiding wasted effort.

1067
00:49:26,880 --> 00:49:29,950
Okay, phrase 16, streamline.

1068
00:49:29,970 --> 00:49:33,390
Think the opposite of chaotic, inefficient, all over the place.

1069
00:49:34,220 --> 00:49:43,420
To streamline means making a process, a system, an operation really effective, efficient, well-organized, cutting out unnecessary steps, making it flow smoothly.

1070
00:49:43,400 --> 00:49:45,900
You might hear, we need to streamline this onboarding process.

1071
00:49:45,960 --> 00:49:47,940
It's taking too long and confusing new hires.

1072
00:49:48,440 --> 00:49:50,550
The goal is to make it simpler, faster, better.

1073
00:49:50,970 --> 00:49:53,110
A streamlined process is logical, predictable.

1074
00:49:53,150 --> 00:49:54,640
Everyone knows what to do, when.

1075
00:49:54,930 --> 00:49:56,420
No wasted steps, no confusion.

1076
00:49:56,520 --> 00:49:56,940
Think about it.

1077
00:49:57,180 --> 00:50:01,110
Instead of random emails and documents everywhere, a streamlined system has a clear sequence.

1078
00:50:01,370 --> 00:50:03,970
That leads to lower costs, faster results, higher quality.

1079
00:50:04,100 --> 00:50:08,450
This raises that vital question of operational excellence, optimization.

1080
00:50:08,890 --> 00:50:10,990
Streamlining is all about process improvement.

1081
00:50:11,230 --> 00:50:15,400
Finding the bottlenecks, cutting the waste, creating that smooth, logical flow you described.

1082
00:50:15,420 --> 00:50:17,000
The example you gave is perfect.

1083
00:50:17,020 --> 00:50:20,140
Instead of chaos, you get clarity, sequence, predictability.

1084
00:50:20,490 --> 00:50:24,750
That directly impacts the bottom line, saving time, saving money, boosting productivity.

1085
00:50:25,090 --> 00:50:27,420
It's a core concept in efficient business management.

1086
00:50:27,500 --> 00:50:28,700
Getting more done with less friction.

1087
00:50:28,940 --> 00:50:30,720
Making things run like a well-oiled machine.

1088
00:50:31,200 --> 00:50:31,660
Exactly.

1089
00:50:32,870 --> 00:50:33,130
17.

1090
00:50:33,150 --> 00:50:38,300
Scalable is super important, especially for startups or anyone looking to grow significantly.

1091
00:50:39,160 --> 00:50:48,670
Scalable describes the ability of a business or idea to grow bigger, handle more customers, increase profits, without needing a proportional increase in costs or effort.

1092
00:50:49,450 --> 00:50:50,410
It's about efficient growth.

1093
00:50:50,970 --> 00:50:55,460
An investor might say, the current business is small, but the model is highly scalable.

1094
00:50:55,760 --> 00:50:57,140
Huge potential.

1095
00:50:57,120 --> 00:50:59,030
My YouTube channel, for example, is scalable.

1096
00:50:59,630 --> 00:51:03,350
I can teach millions of people with roughly the same effort it takes to teach a few thousand.

1097
00:51:04,030 --> 00:51:06,140
A physical classroom isn't scalable in the same way.

1098
00:51:06,960 --> 00:51:11,640
You could also say, her software idea proved scalable and brought massive profits with minimal extra cost.

1099
00:51:12,380 --> 00:51:16,230
It means the structure allows for growth without breaking the bank or overwhelming the system.

1100
00:51:16,390 --> 00:51:21,900
What's fascinating here is how scalable is such a key indicator of long-term potential.

1101
00:51:22,100 --> 00:51:29,010
It's not just about growing, it's about growing efficiently, leveraging what you have to reach more people, generate more revenue without costs spiraling.

1102
00:51:29,470 --> 00:51:32,550
For investors, like you said, scalability is often paramount.

1103
00:51:32,570 --> 00:51:35,300
It signals the potential for big returns.

1104
00:51:35,500 --> 00:51:40,520
It's a really strategic concept for planning, for designing products, for making investment decisions.

1105
00:51:40,940 --> 00:51:45,170
It's about building something that can get big effectively, the holy grail for growth.

1106
00:51:45,220 --> 00:51:47,340
powerful indicator of potential, definitely.

1107
00:51:48,700 --> 00:51:50,660
Okay, number 18 gives us a great visual.

1108
00:51:51,940 --> 00:51:54,230
In the weeds or lost in the weeds.

1109
00:51:54,810 --> 00:51:57,750
Imagine getting tangled up in actual weeds, losing sight of the path.

1110
00:51:58,090 --> 00:52:04,600
It means getting too focused on tiny, often unimportant details and losing track of the main goal, the bigger picture.

1111
00:52:05,200 --> 00:52:05,880
It's easy to do.

1112
00:52:06,580 --> 00:52:10,900
In a meeting, someone might say, okay, let's not get lost in the weeds discussing the font size right now.

1113
00:52:11,220 --> 00:52:13,270
We need to decide on the overall message first.

1114
00:52:13,250 --> 00:52:15,110
It's a way to pull the focus back up.

1115
00:52:15,450 --> 00:52:23,430
If you're running a meeting and people start drilling down into super minor details that aren't relevant yet, you can say, all right, everyone, interesting points, but let's not get lost in the weeds.

1116
00:52:23,470 --> 00:52:25,220
We can tackle those specifics later.

1117
00:52:25,240 --> 00:52:27,120
Let's stick to the main agenda items for now.

1118
00:52:27,530 --> 00:52:28,270
It keeps things moving.

1119
00:52:28,250 --> 00:52:33,560
And connecting this to effective meetings and strategy, this phrase is invaluable.

1120
00:52:33,600 --> 00:52:38,630
It's a polite way to redirect, to say, hey, important maybe, but not now.

1121
00:52:38,710 --> 00:52:40,710
Let's stay focused on the core objective.

1122
00:52:41,450 --> 00:52:46,720
It helps prevent analysis paralysis, where you get so bogged down in detail you never make a decision.

1123
00:52:47,280 --> 00:52:51,210
It keeps the team focused on the strategic priorities, maintains momentum.

1124
00:52:51,870 --> 00:52:54,270
Knowing when to stay high level is crucial.

1125
00:52:54,250 --> 00:52:57,400
Such a diplomatic way to keep things on track.

1126
00:52:57,420 --> 00:53:00,890
Related to that, phrase 19 is circle back.

1127
00:53:00,910 --> 00:53:01,650
Think of drawing a circle.

1128
00:53:01,670 --> 00:53:04,300
You start somewhere, go around, and end up back at the start.

1129
00:53:04,800 --> 00:53:08,310
It means you plan to revisit or return to a topic or question later on.

1130
00:53:08,680 --> 00:53:13,870
often used in meetings when a point is raised that's valid but may be tangential to the current discussion.

1131
00:53:13,890 --> 00:53:16,410
So someone might say, good question about the budget impact.

1132
00:53:16,450 --> 00:53:19,200
Can we circle back to that once we finish discussing the timeline?

1133
00:53:19,540 --> 00:53:21,260
You're acknowledging it but parking it for later.

1134
00:53:21,980 --> 00:53:25,310
Or if someone promised to circle back and forgot, you might gently remind them.

1135
00:53:26,190 --> 00:53:29,490
If you wanted to circle back to my earlier question about the deadline, it's a polite nudge.

1136
00:53:30,080 --> 00:53:30,840
We didn't resolve this.

1137
00:53:30,880 --> 00:53:31,820
Let's return to it.

1138
00:53:31,800 --> 00:53:35,470
And this raises that important point about balancing input with efficiency.

1139
00:53:35,970 --> 00:53:41,210
Circleback lets you acknowledge someone's point, validate it, without letting it derail the meeting's flow.

1140
00:53:41,530 --> 00:53:42,260
It's diplomatic.

1141
00:53:42,780 --> 00:53:48,300
It also gives the person who raised the point some assurance that it will be addressed, just maybe not right this second.

1142
00:53:48,970 --> 00:53:51,230
Fosters inclusivity while maintaining structure.

1143
00:53:51,290 --> 00:53:54,700
It ensures things don't get lost, but keeps the main discussion on track.

1144
00:53:55,180 --> 00:53:56,560
It's a commitment to revisit.

1145
00:53:56,640 --> 00:53:58,360
Great tool for meeting management, for sure.

1146
00:53:59,040 --> 00:54:02,230
And our final phrase before acronyms, number 20, is very similar.

1147
00:54:02,770 --> 00:54:03,650
Put a pin in it.

1148
00:54:04,670 --> 00:54:06,050
Eight, again, great visual.

1149
00:54:06,590 --> 00:54:09,840
Imagine literally sticking a pin in an idea on a notice board.

1150
00:54:10,480 --> 00:54:11,880
You're marking it to come back to later.

1151
00:54:12,420 --> 00:54:17,050
It means acknowledging an idea or topic is important, but you're postponing the discussion.

1152
00:54:17,390 --> 00:54:19,030
Like, circle back, but...

1153
00:54:19,010 --> 00:54:21,840
Maybe feels a little more like we'll definitely put this on a future agenda.

1154
00:54:22,720 --> 00:54:25,620
You might say, that's a really interesting idea about restructuring the team.

1155
00:54:25,900 --> 00:54:28,790
Let's put a pin in it and dedicate time to discuss it properly next week.

1156
00:54:29,570 --> 00:54:32,170
You're validating the idea, but scheduling it for later.

1157
00:54:32,310 --> 00:54:32,890
It communicates.

1158
00:54:33,350 --> 00:54:33,790
Good point.

1159
00:54:34,280 --> 00:54:34,740
Valuable.

1160
00:54:34,820 --> 00:54:35,980
Deserves proper attention.

1161
00:54:36,380 --> 00:54:37,040
But not right now.

1162
00:54:37,080 --> 00:54:37,860
We've noted it down.

1163
00:54:38,420 --> 00:54:39,840
It's a clear commitment to return.

1164
00:54:39,910 --> 00:54:44,650
What's fascinating is how this like circle back validates the contribution while managing the flow.

1165
00:54:45,190 --> 00:54:49,860
It says your idea matters, which is important for morale and encouraging participation.

1166
00:54:50,540 --> 00:54:52,340
It provides that psychological comfort.

1167
00:54:52,660 --> 00:54:54,030
My idea wasn't just ignored.

1168
00:54:54,070 --> 00:54:58,530
It's parked for later discussion and it helps keep the current meeting focused and on agenda.

1169
00:54:58,930 --> 00:55:04,380
It's another smart tool for structured, respectful and efficient discussions prevents good ideas from getting lost.

1170
00:55:04,560 --> 00:55:05,460
Okay.

1171
00:55:05,480 --> 00:55:06,420
20 phrases down.

1172
00:55:07,180 --> 00:55:09,830
Now, as promised, let's get into those acronyms.

1173
00:55:09,870 --> 00:55:12,830
Here's where it gets potentially confusing if you haven't seen them before.

1174
00:55:13,250 --> 00:55:17,460
Five really common ones you'll see in emails, reports, maybe even here sometimes.

1175
00:55:17,700 --> 00:55:18,640
Real shortcuts.

1176
00:55:18,660 --> 00:55:20,760
First one, imagine getting an email.

1177
00:55:21,400 --> 00:55:23,580
Need the report by EOD Wednesday.

1178
00:55:24,200 --> 00:55:25,140
EOD, what's that?

1179
00:55:25,280 --> 00:55:26,670
EOD simply stands for end of day.

1180
00:55:27,030 --> 00:55:27,690
It's a deadline.

1181
00:55:27,830 --> 00:55:30,770
People just write EOD instead of typing out end of day.

1182
00:55:30,750 --> 00:55:35,380
You probably won't hear people say EOD much in conversation, but you will absolutely see it written down all the time.

1183
00:55:35,940 --> 00:55:37,140
Emails, project plans.

1184
00:55:37,540 --> 00:55:40,440
It means get it done before the close of business on that day.

1185
00:55:40,880 --> 00:55:41,080
Right.

1186
00:55:41,200 --> 00:55:42,540
But here's a nuance.

1187
00:55:42,560 --> 00:55:46,000
EOD seems simple, but in a global team, end of whose day?

1188
00:55:46,060 --> 00:55:49,130
The senders, the recipients, the headquarters.

1189
00:55:49,530 --> 00:55:53,230
It actually highlights the need for clarity, especially across time zones.

1190
00:55:53,650 --> 00:55:57,050
What might be 5 p.m. for you could be 2 a.m. for your colleague.

1191
00:55:57,430 --> 00:56:00,660
So while it's a shortcut, you often need that extra context.

1192
00:56:00,640 --> 00:56:04,130
EOD my time or EOD PST.

1193
00:56:04,690 --> 00:56:08,000
Otherwise, the simple acronym can actually cause confusion.

1194
00:56:08,060 --> 00:56:09,580
It makes you think about the specifics.

1195
00:56:09,600 --> 00:56:10,820
That's a fantastic point.

1196
00:56:10,860 --> 00:56:11,770
The time zone trap.

1197
00:56:11,790 --> 00:56:12,830
Definitely need clarity there.

1198
00:56:12,970 --> 00:56:13,450
Yeah.

1199
00:56:13,510 --> 00:56:13,570
OK.

1200
00:56:13,630 --> 00:56:14,310
Acronym number two.

1201
00:56:15,040 --> 00:56:15,660
Oh, oh.

1202
00:56:15,640 --> 00:56:18,080
You've definitely seen this if you use email for work.

1203
00:56:18,380 --> 00:56:20,410
You email someone and get an instant reply back.

1204
00:56:20,690 --> 00:56:22,770
Sorry, I will be 00 until March 10th.

1205
00:56:23,190 --> 00:56:24,990
000, it just means out of office.

1206
00:56:25,350 --> 00:56:25,770
Simple as that.

1207
00:56:26,050 --> 00:56:28,080
For whatever reason, people often use the acronym.

1208
00:56:28,160 --> 00:56:29,360
It tells you they're not available right now.

1209
00:56:29,420 --> 00:56:32,770
Maybe vacation, maybe sick leave, maybe travel, but they'll be back on the date specified.

1210
00:56:33,270 --> 00:56:36,110
Almost always seen in writing, especially those auto replies.

1211
00:56:36,260 --> 00:56:40,360
And what's interesting here is how OKO functions as a professional boundary.

1212
00:56:40,820 --> 00:56:42,650
It manages expectations automatically.

1213
00:56:42,790 --> 00:56:44,950
People know not to expect an immediate reply.

1214
00:56:45,290 --> 00:56:48,630
It tells them who to contact if it's urgent, when the person will be back.

1215
00:56:49,010 --> 00:56:50,760
It's essential for business continuity.

1216
00:56:51,060 --> 00:56:55,480
It standardizes how absences are communicated, allowing everyone else to plan around it.

1217
00:56:55,840 --> 00:57:00,330
A simple acronym that does a lot of work in managing communication flow and expectations.

1218
00:57:00,430 --> 00:57:02,110
Yeah, a little acronym doing heavy lifting there.

1219
00:57:02,470 --> 00:57:05,450
Okay, number three is super common, heard and seen everywhere.

1220
00:57:05,810 --> 00:57:06,870
ASAP.

1221
00:57:07,430 --> 00:57:09,850
ASA stands for as soon as possible.

1222
00:57:10,170 --> 00:57:17,180
You'll hear it pronounced two ways, letter by letter, ASAP, or as a word, ASAP, like ASAP.

1223
00:57:17,200 --> 00:57:20,340
It means now, immediately, highest priority.

1224
00:57:20,680 --> 00:57:22,860
You might say, can you send me those figures ASAP?

1225
00:57:23,180 --> 00:57:25,910
Or write in an email, please review this document ASAP.

1226
00:57:26,250 --> 00:57:27,510
Remember to capitalize it in writing.

1227
00:57:27,630 --> 00:57:28,870
It signals extreme urgency.

1228
00:57:29,010 --> 00:57:30,430
You need to be a bit careful with it, though.

1229
00:57:30,580 --> 00:57:31,240
Exactly.

1230
00:57:31,260 --> 00:57:34,240
This raises that important question about urgency inflation.

1231
00:57:34,860 --> 00:57:38,510
ASAP means now, critical, drop everything.

1232
00:57:38,990 --> 00:57:40,930
If you use it for everything, it loses all meaning.

1233
00:57:41,130 --> 00:57:42,230
People start to ignore it.

1234
00:57:42,630 --> 00:57:44,370
It can also create unnecessary stress.

1235
00:57:44,800 --> 00:57:45,760
So use it wisely.

1236
00:57:45,800 --> 00:57:49,300
Only when something truly is the highest priority and needs immediate action.

1237
00:57:49,900 --> 00:57:53,980
Using it sparingly preserves its power and shows respect for your colleague's workload.

1238
00:57:54,340 --> 00:57:56,810
Don't be the person crying ASAP all the time.

1239
00:57:56,790 --> 00:57:57,410
Good advice.

1240
00:57:57,970 --> 00:58:00,030
The ASAP overload is real.

1241
00:58:00,910 --> 00:58:02,420
OK, fourth acronym, FYI.

1242
00:58:02,460 --> 00:58:06,620
You get an email that says, FYI, the client meeting moved to 2 p.m. FYI.

1243
00:58:07,060 --> 00:58:09,410
FYI stands for for your information.

1244
00:58:09,710 --> 00:58:15,020
It's used when you're passing along a piece of information that the recipient should know, but doesn't necessarily need to act on immediately.

1245
00:58:15,060 --> 00:58:15,640
It's a heads up.

1246
00:58:15,660 --> 00:58:18,500
So that email just means just letting you know the meeting time changed.

1247
00:58:18,860 --> 00:58:22,470
Mostly written, but you might hear sometimes, oh, FYI, I won't be in the office tomorrow afternoon.

1248
00:58:22,950 --> 00:58:24,650
It's a quick, concise way to share info.

1249
00:58:24,630 --> 00:58:29,480
What's neat about FYI is how it enables efficient information sharing without demanding a response.

1250
00:58:30,060 --> 00:58:34,140
It's an update, a bit of context, something relevant the person should be aware of.

1251
00:58:34,540 --> 00:58:38,610
It helps keep people informed without cluttering their inbox with things requiring action.

1252
00:58:39,130 --> 00:58:42,270
Very useful for broadcasting updates or background info to a group.

1253
00:58:42,810 --> 00:58:44,520
Efficient, low-pressure communication.

1254
00:58:44,540 --> 00:58:46,080
The silent workhorse, as you said before.

1255
00:58:46,220 --> 00:58:46,680
Exactly.

1256
00:58:46,760 --> 00:58:47,920
Inform without obligate.

1257
00:58:48,580 --> 00:58:49,540
Perfect.

1258
00:58:49,520 --> 00:58:52,430
And our final acronym, number five, is a fun one.

1259
00:58:52,810 --> 00:58:53,530
TGIF.

1260
00:58:54,330 --> 00:58:57,430
TGIF stands for Thank God It's Friday.

1261
00:58:57,490 --> 00:59:01,240
Or sometimes, maybe a bit more inclusively, Thank Goodness It's Friday.

1262
00:59:01,580 --> 00:59:04,780
It's that universal feeling of relief and happiness at the end of the work week.

1263
00:59:04,820 --> 00:59:05,720
Yay, weekend.

1264
00:59:06,220 --> 00:59:10,550
You'll definitely see this in company chat channels, maybe with a happy emoji or GIF.

1265
00:59:10,530 --> 00:59:11,630
TGIF, everyone.

1266
00:59:11,950 --> 00:59:15,120
It just expresses that shared relief, that anticipation for the weekend.

1267
00:59:15,500 --> 00:59:19,500
Less common to say it out loud formally, but you might hear it informally among colleagues.

1268
00:59:19,840 --> 00:59:20,700
TGIF.

1269
00:59:20,720 --> 00:59:21,300
Have a good weekend.

1270
00:59:21,620 --> 00:59:22,560
Very common to write, though.

1271
00:59:22,720 --> 00:59:27,150
And if we connect this to workplace culture, TGIF is more than just letters.

1272
00:59:27,630 --> 00:59:30,630
It taps into that shared rhythm of the work week.

1273
00:59:30,650 --> 00:59:33,540
The value we place on downtime, on work-life balance.

1274
00:59:33,560 --> 00:59:35,460
It's a collective sigh of relief.

1275
00:59:35,440 --> 00:59:37,890
It's informal, yes, but it builds camaraderie.

1276
00:59:38,490 --> 00:59:44,220
A little shared moment of lightheartedness can actually boost morale, make the workplace feel a bit more human.

1277
00:59:44,280 --> 00:59:48,220
It acknowledges that cycle of work and rest we all experience.

1278
00:59:48,560 --> 00:59:50,240
A weekly mini-celebration.

1279
00:59:50,340 --> 00:59:51,160
Well, there you have it.

1280
00:59:51,180 --> 00:59:54,050
20 essential phrases, five key acronyms.

1281
00:59:54,510 --> 00:59:56,770
You've just equipped yourself with some really powerful tools.

1282
00:59:57,190 --> 00:59:58,270
So what does all this mean for you?

1283
00:59:58,910 --> 01:00:04,480
It means you're better prepared to understand and be understood in the often fast-paced world of business English.

1284
01:00:04,860 --> 01:00:06,280
These aren't just vocabulary lists.

1285
01:00:06,520 --> 01:00:10,930
They're keys to navigating relationships, managing expectations, participating effectively.

1286
01:00:11,450 --> 01:00:12,530
Really fruitful stuff.

1287
01:00:12,580 --> 01:00:13,220
Absolutely.

1288
01:00:13,260 --> 01:00:15,660
And it connects to that goal of learning effectively, right?

1289
01:00:15,840 --> 01:00:17,740
Getting the nuances, not just the basics.

1290
01:00:18,320 --> 01:00:23,310
This whole discussion host really raises that important question about the subtle power of language.

1291
01:00:23,330 --> 01:00:24,970
Mastering these isn't just about grammar.

1292
01:00:25,110 --> 01:00:28,610
It's about confidence, understanding culture, influencing outcomes.

1293
01:00:29,160 --> 01:00:34,700
These phrases are like little windows into workplace values, communication styles, the unwritten rules.

1294
01:00:35,200 --> 01:00:37,400
Grasping them gives you more than just language skills.

1295
01:00:37,480 --> 01:00:40,970
It gives you deeper cultural insight, helps you navigate more effectively.

1296
01:00:41,320 --> 01:00:42,480
Such a critical point.

1297
